\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  Mila can't sleep -- up on the cart, blanket around her shoulders. She doesn't go looking for Bjorn or Saijah specifically; she just sits where both are likely to notice her, and they do. This is Saijah's and Alfonso's scene as much as Bjorn's -- Mila has come to think of them as something like a second set of parents since Forelhost, and this beat is built to use that, not skip past it.\f2\lang1033  [had this run before the ismara scene because the player had to step out, bjorn didnt see her first, saijah did and went to comfort her but it was saihjah alfonso, anyway they talked to her blah blah and gave her some gifts, ismara bought mila some toys and stuff at the merchants tents a stuffed rabbit toy and a toy sword, anyway this ended up being a mila and siajah scene isntead of a bjorn one, they went and found bjorn drunk with mable and the black mare, balthazzar, he was just shit faced out there so mila and saijah went to sleep by the fire eventually mila fell asleep this happend after saijahs scene with varon which played first in this sequence, but it happened before saijah bough tthe skooma which happened right before davinias stuff.]\f0\lang9\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "Bjorn sees her first and comes to sit, not saying anything for a while -- he's kept a careful distance since she asked why he feels like more than one person, and tonight he closes it a little. 'I made a promise once,' he says finally. 'Broke it. Carried that forty years.' He looks at her, not away. 'Not doing that again.' It's the voice he uses for the things that matter, which is not a voice he uses often.\line\line Saijah's the one who notices Mila's hands are shaking, if anyone does -- close enough by the cart to see it before Bjorn does."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 SAIJAH'S OPENING (player-driven):\b0  what she does with that -- says something, moves closer, lets Alfonso speak, or just stays quiet and present -- is entirely the player's call. If Alfonso speaks, give him something real and in his own voice: he understands promises and their cost better than anyone at this fire, and he isn't sentimental about saying so.\par

\pard\sa140 Bjorn gets up and shows Mila the mare instead of finishing the moment with words -- left side, breathing even, hands open. The black mare, unbothered by almost everything, suffers the careful child where she suffers nobody else. Mila brushes her. Somewhere in the middle of it her hands stop shaking, and whoever's watching -- Bjorn, Saijah, both -- gets to actually be there for it instead of just narrated at.\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf5\b\f1\fs20 DOWNTIME OPTION -- FORAGING (any PC, before dark; optional)\f3\lang1033  [no forraging done]\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\lang9\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 Its own d20 roll, no skill, no stat (Ingredient Foraging Map). GENERAL: roll d20 on the Rift table below, get 1 of whatever the number says. TARGETED (hunting a specific ingredient they know grows here): find its number N on the table; roll at or under N to find it, and quantity = (N - roll) + 1. Roll over N = an empty look-around, or GM's call to hand them the rolled entry instead.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 THE RIFT -- FORAGE d20 (low = rarer find)\b0\line  1 Hagraven Feather   6 Falmer Ear       11 Honeycomb          16 Garlic\line  2 Vampire Dust       7 Canis Root       12 Blue Butterfly Wing 17 Thistle Branch\line  3 Ectoplasm          8 Mora Tapinella   13 Butterfly Wing      18 Nightshade\line  4 Nirnroot           9 Nordic Barnacle  14 River Betty         19 Blue Mountain Flower\line  5 Luna Moth Wing    10 Bee              15 Blisterwort         20 Wheat\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 THE CARAVAN BREAKS CAMP -- Ahkari's goodbye (morning; run it short)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "Half the tents are already down, the rugs rolled, the children loading. Ahkari crosses over with a tin the size of a fist and a folded square of paper.\line\line 'Put the purse away! No, no -- this one is not selling this morning. Take the tin, and take the paper too -- the paper is the making of it, written plain, so you can make more when the tin is empty. Yours now.'\line\line 'And you come back, yes? Ahkari's little ones have been playing at being you since the story, and they will want more of it than this one has to tell them. So find the fires again, and bring Ahkari something new.'"\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  AHKARI IS THE SUPPLIER. Per the play record in Scene 3, she sold Saijah the skooma, watched her take moon sugar and skooma at the fair, and sold her THREE MORE BOTTLES immediately after the first dose. She knows precisely what she sold and to whom, and a caravan trader who moves skooma has seen what three-bottles-at-once means before. Whether this gift is an ordinary trader's thank-you to good patrons, or a dealer quietly handing a customer she likes the thing that holds off the worst of it, is YOURS to aim -- she does not name it either way, and Saijah is not mentioned.\par
\cf1\b GM (play her from the religion, not from Skyrim's gutter framing):\cf2\b0  moon sugar is SACRED to the Khajiit -- "a gift from the moons themselves," central to Khajiit religious practice and to healing rites (Lore Books/Gods and Demons). Ahkari sells it the way it is meant to be sold, openly, without shame or hedging, and her own fireside story is built on the same theology -- "every gift the moons ever braided into our people," "the moons that made him made every cub at this fire" (Scene 3). She is not a back-alley dealer being coy. This caravan being the easiest place in the campaign to find sugar or skooma is DELIBERATE and load-bearing design: the Khajiit road IS the supply line, permanently, and Saijah now knows exactly where it runs and that Ahkari will be back on it.\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20\b THE TIN:\b0  refined moon sugar. Set the dose count when you know how long the mountain runs. Each dose HALVES all FP costs for 20 real minutes, and suppresses skooma dependence penalties for 24 in-fiction hours WITHOUT advancing the dose counter (Alchemy GM Reference Sec. 11).\line \b THE PAPER -- this is the actual gift:\b0  the written recipe. Per the Alchemy GM Reference, refining is gated on KNOWLEDGE alone (somebody has to teach the process or work it out); the recipe clears that gate PERMANENTLY. From this morning the party's alchemist can produce refined moon sugar from raw moon sugar with no roll, forever -- and moon sugar is ordinary open caravan stock (Scene 3).\line \b WHY THAT IS DANGEROUS:\b0  Saijah's supply problem is now solved by her own friends. It degrades on consecutive days (2nd day steps the penalties down one stage only; 3rd day suppresses nothing), a full clean day resets it, and SEVEN consecutive days sets its own habit (-2 to all Stat Scores on any day without it; two clean days breaks it). It buys a mountain crossing. It cannot buy thirty days clean.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb100\sa240\cf1\b EXIT ->\cf2\b0  Morning. The open stretch.   \b -> 5  THE HIDE\f2\lang1033  pick up here. siajh got addicted to skooma \b0\f0\lang9\par

\pard\sb280\sa40\cf1\b\fs40 5   THE SHADOW ON THE SNOW -- the hide\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa220\cf3\i The set-piece. Two dragons work a logging cut like a search team, a child and an old man in the cart. Being seen is not the danger -- being recognized is. The party that killed two dragons has to convince two more they are only frightened mortals. [BATTLEMAP NEEDED -- the logging cut: road, cart, slash piles, stumps, the hide. GM is drawing this one himself.]\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 THE TERRAIN (give the table ALL of it -- their plans are built from this)\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa80\cf5\b\f1\fs20\bullet\tab\cf2 THE CUT:\b0  a quarter-mile-wide gap clear-felled through the pines for a Riften timber crew's haul road -- stumps waist-high in rows, the ground churned to mud and old sawdust, the sky wide open overhead for the only stretch of this whole road. The party is a third of the way across it when the shadows come.\f0\fs24\par
\cf5\b\f1\fs20\bullet\tab\cf2 THE STUMP ROWS (either side):\b0  waist-high, staggered. Not real cover from directly overhead, but they break up the ground enough to lie flat between them and read as terrain-noise instead of a person, if nobody moves.\f0\fs24\par
\cf5\b\f1\fs20\bullet\tab\cf2 THE FLUME DITCH:\b0  a log-flume cut, dug to slide timber downhill to the river, three feet deep, dry this season. A person can lie full-length in it, hidden from above; the cart cannot fit.\f0\fs24\par
\cf5\b\f1\fs20\bullet\tab\cf2 THE SLASH PILE (north edge, near the treeline):\b0  cut branches and bark heaped for burning -- loose, and it WILL shift and rattle if handled carelessly (Scene 6 uses this).\f0\fs24\par
\cf5\b\f1\fs20\bullet\tab\cf2 THE CART:\b0  canvas over bows, dirty grey-brown -- from altitude, still, with its fire-colors hidden, it can read as one more slash heap. MOVEMENT is what kills: wheels turning, animals shifting, faces looking up.\f0\fs24\par
\cf5\b\f1\fs20\bullet\tab\cf2 THE TREELINE\b0 , close on both sides: real cover, but forty yards off across open ground -- a long way to run under two dragons' eyes.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf4\b\i TODO -- BATTLEMAP (GM is drawing this himself):\cf2\b0  the whole scene is a positioning puzzle, so the table needs to SEE it. Required features: the quarter-mile cut running east-west with the haul road through it; the staggered waist-high stump rows on both sides; the dry flume ditch three feet deep along the NORTH edge (cart does not fit); the slash pile at the north edge near the treeline (Scene 6 drops this on Mila); the cart's position a third of the way across; and the treeline forty yards off on BOTH sides. Mark the forty-yard runs, since every plan the table makes is priced in how far it is to cover.\i0\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "The road threads out of the pines into open sky -- a logging cut, a quarter mile of stumps and churned mud where a Riften crew has been felling timber for the haul road. Waist-high stumps stagger in rows on either side, a dry log-flume ditch runs three feet deep along the north edge, and cut branches are heaped into a slash pile near the treeline. The trees themselves stand forty yards off on both sides, pulled back for the first time all day.\line\line You're a third of the way across it when the sun goes dark. A shadow crosses the mud ahead of the cart, vast and wing-shaped. There's a sound with it, wingbeats bigger than sails, tearing the air in long strokes -- the same sound you heard over Kynesgrove, when the village burned. A second shadow crosses the first.\line\line Two of them.\line\line They wheel out over the cut, unhurried, patient, reading the ground. They caught a glimpse of something down here, and now they're circling to be sure."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sa140\b FRAME THE STAKES OUT LOUD, THEN STEP BACK:\b0  say it plainly -- you fought one dragon and barely survived; there are two, in the open, with a child and an old man in the cart; there is no version of a fight here that ends well. Then stop talking. If the table decides to try anyway, that's a real choice they're allowed to make -- run it honestly, don't fudge it soft, and let the terrain and the odds be the actual argument instead of you overriding their agency with narration.\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 HOW THE TWO OF THEM SEARCH (intelligent hunters, not animals)\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 YOLSHULNIR (fire, male) and STRUNBAHNIR (storm, female) -- hatched together, hunting together -- work the cut like a spec-ops pair: one holds the sky as overwatch while the other drops to check by claw and scent. They SPEAK, calm and exact, in Dovahzul the party cannot understand. They hunt one thing -- a SOUL-THIEF, the residue of a stolen dragon soul. CANON SHIELD: a Dragonborn cannot be sensed; they physically cannot smell what Bjorn is. Only BEHAVIOR betrays the party -- acting like the thing that kills dragons instead of frightened prey.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 THE HIDE -- an invisible 18-point tally\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 The challenge is not being unseen -- it is not being RECOGNIZED. A hidden 0-18 tally the table never sees; it resolves only after the sixth beat. SIX investigation beats; all THREE PCs (Davinia, Saijah, Orion) roll each beat to read as frightened prey -- 18 rolls, 18 points. Difficulty climbs as the dragons close: Beat 1 Easy(+2), 2 Easy(+2), 3 Standard(0), 4 Hard(-4), 5 Hard(-4), 6 Very Hard(-6).\f0\fs24\par
\f1\fs20 SCORING (tug-of-war): each FAILED roll +1 toward recognition; a genuinely clever or debasing prey-move -1; anything RECKLESS, defiant, or showing power +2 to +3. OVERT AGGRESSION (attacking, an unmistakable shout) OR ALFONSO TAKING A BODY (the Tenancy -- a soul moving into another host) MAXES the tally instantly: a soul crossing bodies is the exact anomaly they hunt. RESOLUTION (still hidden until now): 10 or more -- the dragons RECOGNIZE them, throats light, flight-or-death, TPK earned. 9 or fewer -- they conclude it is nothing and leave for the Golden-Wood (THE WORD, Scene 6).\f0\fs24\par
\f1\fs20 BEATS 1-2 run here (aerial); the low pass (beat 3) opens Scene 6, where the flare, the ground search (beats 4-6), and the resolution all land. The dragons speak to each other as they work -- the party hears only Dovahzul.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 BEAT 1 -- THE SHADOW SWEEP (Easy):\b0  Strunbahnir drags her shadow across their ground, testing for a flinch. STRUNBAHNIR: "Naan folaas het." (Something is wrong here.) Yolshulnir turns toward where she is already looking.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 BEAT 2 -- THE HOLD (Easy):\b0  she takes overwatch, he goes to work. STRUNBAHNIR: "Zu'u dein lok. Bo tum." (I hold the sky. Fly down.)\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  \b ESBERN\b0 , low: "That's a search pattern. Wide, methodical. Alduin's raising his kin and sending them out ahead of him to look." He does NOT know their target; keep the dread general.\par

\pard\sb100\sa240\cf1\b EXIT ->\cf2\b0  Beat 3 -- the near dragon drops into its low pass, and its downdraft is what drops the slash pile.   \b -> 6  BJORN'S SECOND FLARE\b0\par

\pard\sb280\sa40\cf1\b\fs40 6   BJORN'S SECOND FLARE -- the worst moment\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "The near dragon drops low across the cut, working the ground the way a hawk works a field. The downdraft hits the cart broadside and rocks it up onto two wheels. Mila goes with it, sliding out of the bed toward the open side.\line\line And the slash pile lets go. Cut branches come down the heap in a grinding slide, a season of dead timber, onto the ground she is sliding toward."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 (This low pass is BEAT 3, Standard -- the PCs roll to stay prey as it happens. What follows is the flare, then the ground search, beats 4-6.)\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa140 For one impossible instant Bjorn crosses ground no man his age should be able to cross -- twenty feet of it, from the rear of the cart, and he is between her and the timber when it comes down -- and it doesn't feel strange to him at all. It feels like the easiest thing he's done all week. His eyes stay on Mila the whole time, checking her over once, fast, the way he's checked her a hundred times before, already breathing like it's over.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  THE BIND (the flare feeds the tally): an old man crossing that ground is exactly the not-a-frightened-mortal thing the dragons key on. Saving Mila ADDS points to the invisible tally -- and the table never sees the number it just spiked. Save the child, risk everyone; the cruelty is that they cannot know.\par
\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  WHAT THE TABLE MAY CONCLUDE: the old mercenary is reviving -- youth, speed, the War-Hound returning. A rejuvenation. Eerie. That is ALL. The Dragonborn truth does NOT surface and must not leak (his FIRST flare was the Ratway stairs, Ch.21 -- one prior data point, still no word for it). Davinia, through Ismara's Sight, sees the doubled sun of his soul flare bright and bank down -- one more silent data point. Hold the secret.\par

\pard\sa140\b BJORN\b0  (gruff, not meeting anyone's eye): "...Adrenaline. You'd be quick too, with that thing overhead." (already turning away) "Get her back under. Nobody moves."\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 BEAT 4 -- HE LANDS (Hard):\b0  the air search doesn't resolve it; Yolshulnir commits to the ground, claws finding the mud. Strunbahnir stays up. YOLSHULNIR: "Bo tum." (Descending.)\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 BEAT 5 -- CLAWS THROUGH THE STUMPS (Hard):\b0  he rakes a row apart at close range, tasting for theft where a person might lie.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 BEAT 6 -- THE CLOSEST CHECK (Very Hard):\b0  the last, most invasive look -- the cart itself, the one shape Strunbahnir cannot read from above. Then the tally resolves.\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 THE WORD -- if the party stayed prey (tally 9 or under)\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa120\cf6\i YOLSHULNIR, lifting his head from the stumps: "Nid siltafiir het. Nunon zofaas joorre."\cf2\i0\par
\cf6\i STRUNBAHNIR, from overwatch: "Ruz joorre los vobalaan. Yuvonfeykro saraan. Fin tafiir bo til."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  MEANING (the party understands NONE of it): "No soul-thief here. Only frightened mortals." / "Then the mortals are beneath us. The Golden-Wood awaits. The thief walks there." Yuvonfeykro = the dragons' name for the Rift/Riften. This is the Fall of Riften seed -- carried only by you.\par
\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  THE DIRECTION THEY LEAVE IN (only now do they go): the dragons bank away EAST, down the long line of the valley they just climbed out of, toward the Rift. If a player asks which way that is, give the compass point ONLY -- east, back down the road they came up -- and stop there. Do not name the city. A player tracking their own route out of Riften can assemble the dread themselves. (The same fact as Yuvonfeykro, from the party's side: they get the direction, never the word.)\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD  (they go)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "Yolshulnir lifts his head out of the stumps and holds still a moment, the way a dog does when it has lost a scent it was certain of. Then he pushes off the churned ground hard enough to throw mud thirty feet, and climbs.\line\line Strunbahnir is already turning. She takes the height first, and he comes up under her, and they fall into the same line without either of them looking at the other.\line\line They go east, down the long valley you climbed out of this morning, until the two shapes are small and then are gone. The cut is very quiet. Somewhere behind you the slash pile is still settling."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sa140\i Esbern breaks the quiet first, once it's safe to talk.\i0\par
\b ESBERN\b0 : "Two of them, and we walked under them blind." (he taps the side of the cart, hard) "Alduin is raising them out of their own burial mounds. The Dragonstone maps every one of those mounds in Skyrim." (a beat) "With it in my hands I would know where the next one comes up. I would know before it did."\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf6\b\f1\fs20 IF RECOGNIZED (tally 10+) -- FULL COMBAT, NOT A PUZZLE\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 No WORD, no departure -- the nearer throat lights from within. TELEGRAPH IT: describe the throat igniting, give one full beat for anyone to declare they're running, and do not fudge the first hit soft if they stand. This is not Krosulhah -- there is no avalanche-equivalent lever in an open logging cut, no chokepoint, nothing to collapse. That absence is the design. If the table commits to a fight after Scene 5's stakes were framed plainly, the commitment is real and this is what it costs.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 EACH DRAGON ROLLS ITS OWN INITIATIVE -- NOT a shared enemy slot. Two independent apex actors interleaved with the party's three is what makes this lethal; do not simplify it into one combined turn.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 YOLSHULNIR -- FIRE DRAGON (APEX)\b0\line HP 900, in TWO PHASE BARS of 450 (damage does NOT carry between bars)\line DR 22   Immune: Fear, Poison, Disease, Fire\line ATTACKS:\line   Inferno Breath | Major, 40ft cone | -6 Agi | 40 fire fail / 20 success\line   Bite           | Major, melee | 45 phys | Prone + Grapple\line   Wildfire Claws | Major, melee | 30 phys | ignites the ground beneath the target\line TERRAIN IGNITION (automatic, no roll): this cut is dry stumps, old sawdust, and a\line slash pile built for burning. Any time Inferno Breath, Wildfire Claws, or a Fanned\line Inferno (below) lands anywhere in the cut, that ground catches -- a standing fire\line hazard, 5 fire/round to anyone in it, no check required. The terrain wants to burn.\line GROWING HUNGER (passive): at the start of his turn, if ANY fire is burning anywhere\line on the field -- his own or hers via Fanned Inferno -- he regains 10 HP. He does not\line resist fire. He is fed by it, regardless of who lit it.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 STRUNBAHNIR -- STORM DRAGON (APEX)\b0\line HP 900, in TWO PHASE BARS of 450 (damage does NOT carry between bars)\line DR 22   Immune: Fear, Poison, Disease, Shock\line ATTACKS:\line   Storm Call    | Major, ranged 60ft, any point she can see, no breath-cone -- a bolt\line                 called down out of the sky, not exhaled | -6 Agi | 40 shock fail / 20\line                 success | ignores half DR from metal armor | usable at full altitude\line   Storm Fracture| Major, ranged 60ft, one point she can see -- the anchor bolt hits\line                 full force (as Storm Call), then shatters outward: everyone else within\line                 30ft of that point takes 15 shock / -2 Agi. Punishes clustering.\line   Bite          | Major, melee | 45 phys | Prone + Grapple (only if she grounds)\line   Gale Slam     | Major, 20ft radius downdraft | -4 Might | 15 bludg + push 15ft |\line                 snuffs small fires in the radius\line STORM GATHERING (automatic, no roll): every Storm Call or Storm Fracture she USES --\line hit or miss, the discharge itself is what counts -- ionizes the air a little more. At\line 3 uses, a real storm forms overhead, unprompted, and stays for the rest of the fight.\line From the round after it forms: at the start of her turn, she regains 10 HP, same as\line her brother, for the same reason -- she is not resisting the storm, she built it.\line PASSIVE: Advantage resisting Prone/stagger (steady in her own wind).\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 FANNED INFERNO (their signature, not a special trigger -- it just happens if they're\line played as the coordinated pair Scene 5 already establishes): if either of Strunbahnir's\line lightning attacks lands on ground Yolshulnir has already ignited, or Gale Slam pushes a\line target into a Wildfire zone, that fire zone's damage doubles this round and its area\line expands 10ft. Two minds working together, not two stat blocks running in parallel.\line\line\b ALFONSO BURNS.\b0  Dragonfire melts iron, and the rapier is his body -- his DR is his\line hit points and there is nowhere near enough of it. If he is anywhere in the fire he is\line SHATTERED: permanent, irreversible, no re-housing (The Sentient Sword, Sec. III).\line He does not know he is vulnerable this way and neither does whoever is carrying him.\line Everyone else at least gets a new character sheet.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 THE ONLY MERCY VALVE: Bjorn's speed. After the flare above it reads as something\line past adrenaline, and it is the one thing in this cut fast enough to physically carry\line Mila -- and, if the table plays it well, one more -- clear to the treeline before a\line second breath lands. This is not a win condition. It is who gets out.\line\line\b THE SECRET, IN THIS BRANCH ONLY:\b0  everywhere else in this module the Dragonborn\line truth stays buried and must not leak. Here it does not stay buried. If the party\line dies in this cut the campaign continues with NEW Lanterns, and the jump forward\line is real -- Bjorn has to rebuild every one of these relationships from nothing. What\line carries the thread is EVIDENCE, not a confession: a dragon named him aloud and the\line ground bears it out, flattened stumps and a dragon knocked onto its back. He does\line not understand what he did as Shouting, and he cannot explain it or confirm it for\line anyone -- whoever comes next inherits the same question this party did, evidence\line and rumor, not an answer. The party SEES it happen -- read the block below when they\line are down and dying. Do NOT let this bleed into any other branch.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf4\b\fs26 THE LAST THING THEY SEE (TPK branch only -- read when the PCs are down)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  The PCs are down. Everyone still standing is an NPC, and none of them will leave the cart. Bjorn has been rooted in front of it since this started -- he has not gone anywhere and he will not, because he is still the War-Hound and they are not taking the child while he is alive. He does not know how to Shout. It comes out of him anyway.\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "You are down. From where you are lying, this is what you can see.\line\line Varon and Ylva have put themselves between the cart and the dragons. GEAR stands with them, both hands open. Nora is behind them with Mila's face pressed into her shoulder, so the child is looking at the trees.\line\line Bjorn is in front of all of them, in the same spot he has held since this started, unarmed. His hands are shaking.\line\line What comes out of him is louder than anything a man's chest holds. The mud flattens in a line running out from his boots. The stumps snap off at the root all down that line. It catches Yolshulnir where he stands and drives him off his feet, and he goes down through the slash pile with the whole heap coming apart under him.\line\line Strunbahnir turns her head. She holds thirty feet up, wings working to keep her there, and she looks at the old man who drives your cart.\line\line Then she says one word."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sa140\b STRUNBAHNIR:\b0  "Dovahkiin!"\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "Yolshulnir is climbing up out of the broken pile. Both of them have turned toward Bjorn."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  End the READ-ALOUD there -- the dying PCs never see how it resolves. What they take with them, in their last instant, is the flattened ground, a grounded dragon on its back, and the word -- and the word is not ambiguous to anyone at that table.\par
\cf1\b GM ONLY -- WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENS (for continuity; the table never sees this):\cf2\b0  Bjorn finishes it. Both Yolshulnir and Strunbahnir die in this cut. Both souls erupt and go into him the same way Sahloknir's and Krosulhah's already did (Npcs/Bjorn: "the soul erupted... shot down the road toward where Bjorn was") -- he absorbs BOTH at once. The next campaign starts with Bjorn carrying FOUR dragon souls, not two.\par
\cf1\b GM ONLY -- THE HUNT, RECONCILED:\cf2\b0  no dragon survives to identify him -- nothing hunts Bjorn BY NAME, because nobody who saw him lived to report it. But the species-level hunt does not end: dragons already track an abstract soul-thief signature without ever witnessing the theft directly (this scene's own MEANING note above -- "No soul-thief here... Then the mortals are beneath us," Yuvonfeykro). Four dragons gone now, all the same region, is a far louder version of the same signature. The hunt for the soul-thief escalates. It still never resolves to a name.\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 [Open production idea: a simple regional map prop for this beat -- the party's position, the road, and the dragons' heading drawn on it -- would let a sharp player do the orientation themselves. Also flagged: a battlemap for the logging cut, possibly with small animated fly-over moments. Neither built in this pass.]\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb100\sa240\cf1\b EXIT ->\cf2\b0  The shadows shrink east and are gone. That night, or held for later -- the Reckoning.   \b -> THE REVELERS, then THE RECKONING\b0\par

\pard\sb280\sa40\cf1\b\fs40 THE REVELERS -- the last easy hour (early that same night, free-form)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa220\cf3\i Not an encounter. No mechanics unless the table asks for one. This is the last easy air anyone gets before Ivarstead -- let it breathe, and let it run its course naturally back to camp before the Reckoning fires later the same night.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "Off through the trees, firelight flickers where there shouldn't be one -- and singing, off-key and joyful. A handful of travelers have a small fire going, wineskins passed hand to hand. Bjorn's already among them, and so are Ylva, Nora, and Varon, cups in hand, close enough that camp is still visible through the trunks.\line\line Mila's up on Bjorn's shoulders, clapping along to a beat the singers aren't quite managing to hold."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  Esbern knows exactly what's going on tonight. He just didn't feel like it -- stayed at camp. GEAR wasn't invited. These are just regular people having a good night; nothing to run beyond ambiance unless someone specifically wants a mechanic (a dance, a drinking contest -- improvise it if it comes up). Free-form from here: let the table drink, dance, sit it out, whatever they want, for as long as it's fun.\par
\cf1\b GM (the turn):\cf2\b0  As it winds down naturally, everyone drifts back to the main camp for the night -- nothing forced, just travelers calling it. Once the party is back and settled at their own fire, run THE RECKONING below. Nobody at that fire has any idea what's about to happen fifty feet into the dark.\par

\pard\sb280\sa40\cf1\b\fs40 THE RECKONING -- Valerius comes in person\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa220\cf3\i TIMING: LOCKED, this road-camp -- the one after the Hide, before Ivarstead. (Originally meant for the Khajiit caravan night; the session ended before reaching it, so it lands here instead.) Not a fight. Not survivable as one. This is NOT a public confrontation -- read the staging carefully.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sa140 Seeded by Orion disobeying the Constance-enthrall order (Ch.24). Valerius Caelus, \b AP 30\b0 , arrives IN PERSON that night -- a vampire lord travels fast. Not a fight, not a save, and the party cannot intervene: that is the point. \b He does not come to the fire. He takes Orion FROM it.\b0  The Contest Level-Gap Penalty (\b -20 to -25\b0  on any will-vs-will resistance) is the wall, and the rules back it.\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE PULL:\b0  at the fire, the Ring goes from familiar cold to a KILLING cold with a will behind it. Orion does not decide to stop; he simply stops, rises, and walks from the firelight into the dark -- compelled, a thing on a string. The camp watches him go and cannot stop it. \b This is the only part the rest of the party witnesses directly.\b0  The interrogation and the trim happen at the treeline, out of earshot, by design.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE TREELINE:\b0  Valerius is already waiting there.\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i Still tall, close to six and a half feet. Dressed without a wrinkle or a loose thread anywhere on him. Dark hair, cut close, combed back exactly as it was at Falkreath. Pale skin, entirely unblemished.\line\line His shoulders are wider than they were. The muscle across his forearms and chest is new since Falkreath -- real mass, visible through his clothes. His neck is thicker.\line\line He wears a coat of dark blue wool so finely woven the weave itself can't be seen, trimmed at the collar and cuffs with white fur, unbroken but for small dark tips at the very ends of each hair. A gold clasp closes it at the throat, the engraving on its face worn smooth from years of handling. A heavy gold signet ring sits on his right hand, its surface scratched from ordinary wear.\line\line The coat, the clasp, the ring -- these things seem familiar. You have seen them before.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM (clothing tell):\cf2\b0  this is Torygg's own coat, clasp, and signet ring -- casual wear, not state regalia. The worn engraving and scratched ring face are real personal effects, not commissioned copies. Don't name him. Let the table place it, or call for a Guile roll if nobody does.\par
\cf1\b GM (the one real weakness):\cf2\b0  Valerius's love for his dead wife is the one real, unweaponized thing left in him. Everything else -- the courtship of Elisif, the handling of Orion, the courtesy he shows the whole table -- is instrument, calculated, worn like the coat. His wife is not. If her name is spoken in front of him, or his son's, his hands stop moving, whatever they're doing. His jaw sets. He does not speak for three seconds, sometimes four, before he answers. This is real and repeatable -- it fires every time a player finds it, not just once, and it does not close over. Not with time. Not with power. Not ever.\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE INTERROGATION:\b0  he does not ask where they're going.\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i He doesn't announce himself. He is simply there when Orion reaches the treeline, standing among the trees the way a man stands in his own home, and he watches Orion cross the last few feet of ground before he speaks at all.\line\line "You look well," he says, and it isn't courtesy -- it's an actual observation. "Considering." A slow study of him, unhurried, the way a man checks a tool he hasn't handled in a while, confirming the edge has held. Then, without any shift in tone: "Tell me about the Dunmer."\line\line His eyes stay on Orion's face a moment longer than the question needs. "I have watched this before, longer ago than you would believe. A mind poured into a form too small to hold it, serving something that feeds on exactly that kind of failure. He is not losing himself by accident, Orion. Something wants him to lose himself." He leans in, fractionally -- the first time all night his stillness has broken. "Has he said which one. Has anything spoken to him, from inside that iron?"\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  decide live what Orion actually knows and volunteers about Alfonso's condition -- including whether last session's Namira contact ("become less to be more") comes up. This is a real choice for Orion's player, not a foregone reveal. Whatever he learns here, Valerius BANKS -- this is not something the table hears confirmed in the moment; they're still back at the fire.\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i "The archer." His tone resets, unhurried. "One of yours already carries Hircine's mark -- the Cleaver, isn't that what she calls herself. He rarely stops at one." A pause. "Has he made her an offer yet? And more importantly -- has she accepted?"\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  a real contest, not narrated fiat -- have Orion's player roll Guile against the Contest Level-Gap Penalty (-20 to -25). It will almost certainly fail, and that's the point this early: the gap is real, but it's a roll, and a critical success is rarely, genuinely possible. What's contested is whether Valerius gets an answer at all -- if Orion somehow holds, Valerius simply doesn't learn it tonight, and notices the resistance for what it is.\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i He takes that in with no change in his face. "A god of the hunt marked her once, and she refused him. Good. See that it stays refused, if the offer is ever raised to her again. Discourage it, quietly, the way you'd talk a friend out of anything. I don't need to tell you how." A pause. "A wolf does not sicken the way a woman does. I would rather she stayed a woman a while longer."\line\line Something in the set of his shoulders eases, fractionally -- the way a man relaxes once a plan he was prepared to rush no longer needs rushing. "There was a child I had been patient about, for entirely different reasons. It seems I can go on being patient." He leaves the rest unsaid, and moves on.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i "My cousin. When you go back to her --" A pause, then he finishes it plainly. "What will you say to her, exactly?"\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  he is not fishing for what she is -- that stays reserved for himself, see THE MEETING below. This is a loyalty test on ORION: how much independent care does the tool have left. He's not looking for specific content -- a cold, minimal answer ("I'll say you asked to see her, nothing more") reads as a properly broken asset and satisfies him; he simply moves on. Anything showing genuine intent to warn or protect her is a real mark against Orion's obedience -- not punished here, tonight, but remembered, and it should shape how much Valerius trusts him from here forward. Orion's actual answer is entirely the player's.\par
\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  He does not ask about the old man with the cart -- underestimates him completely. His blind spots protect what he genuinely doesn't know (the Crown's true fate, Bjorn's wrongness, what Davinia truly is).\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i He moves to the next thing without any change in pace. "There are two men in this company who belong to someone besides me, and both of them believe they have some say in what happens to you." A brief pause, deciding which to take first. "The old one you hid under the floor. A Blade, if the coat and the manner are honest. What has he actually confirmed about what's coming?"\line\line "Watch him. Whatever he learns, whatever he decides, I want to know before he acts on it."\line\line "Your Brotherhood's man." A different register now -- instruction, not inquiry. "Use him. Whatever leverage the two of you hold over each other, spend it, and put yourself in front of the woman who runs your order. Astrid, I believe the name is." A pause. "I want to know her."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  ESBERN: a standing task, not a one-time question -- Orion is now expected to keep watch on him going forward and report back. VARON: not framed as a threat to neutralize but as a door -- Orion is directed to leverage whatever he holds over Varon (or Varon over him) to gain access to Astrid, expanding Valerius's network into the Dark Brotherhood itself, the same logic as everything else he's built. Orion's actual answers to both remain the player's.\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE TRIM:\b0\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i "A hand that will not close when I will it is not a hand." Valerius does not move.\line\line Orion's right hand closes around the hilt of his own belt knife -- the small one, for eating and skinning -- slowly, deliberately.\line\line His off-arm lifts, turns, lays itself bare below the elbow. The knife rises. "Close it," Valerius says. "Now."\line\line The knife comes down the first time. It goes in clean through skin and the muscle under it and stops there. His knees stay locked. His jaw stays shut.\line\line The knife comes down a second time and finds bone. The shock of it travels up into his shoulder, hard enough to put a man on the ground where he stands. His knees hold. Whatever is holding them is not him.\line\line The knife comes down a third time. The bone gives, and there is a sound that is unmistakably his arm coming apart. His jaw stays shut through that as well.\line\line Then it is over. The will lets go of his knees and his voice in the same instant, and gives both of them back to him at once.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  what Orion actually does now -- sound, words, how he holds himself -- is the player's entirely. Don't narrate the reaction for them.\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i "This is mercy, Orion." Quiet, once he can hear it. "I am keeping the rest of you. Serve better."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20\b MECH (permanent until remedied):\b0  the off-hand is gone; ANY Minor Action requiring the hands becomes a MAJOR Action until Orion has a prosthesis. Movement and speech are the only Minor Actions left to him. No save.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 THE DESIGN\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i He lets Orion feel the wound a moment before he speaks again.\line\line "You spent a night with the girl. Nora." A pause -- the care of a man choosing his next words with precision. "Eighteen, I believe. Alfonso's apprentice."\line\line He says nothing further about her. He does not need to.\line\line "You will serve me well from here forward," he says, and it is not a demand -- only the observation of a fact newly true.\line\line "You believe I sent you for one woman. I did not." A pause. "The woman was a key." A pause. "A house full of children who go where they like, hear everything, and are looked at by no one -- an ear inside Riften that never sleeps and never has to be paid. Constance Michel was the hand that would have turned it to me. I did not ask you to kill her. I asked you to keep her. Had you done that one small thing, the children would be safe today. Now they will die. You and the Dunmer saw to that."\line\line "I have some two hundred people, at this moment, performing small tasks for me across three holds. Each is told only enough to finish his own piece. None of them know the others exist; none of them know what the whole becomes. You were one such piece. You decided, on your own, that yours was too small to matter, and you did not finish it."\line\line "Do you understand what you made me do here tonight?" He seems to want an answer, this time. "I take no pleasure in it. I never have. But if I let one piece decide, quietly, that its part did not matter -- what does the next one decide? And the one after that?" A slow shake of the head. "Everything I have built comes apart in my own hands, one small permission at a time." A pause. "You did this, Orion."\line\line He steps closer.\line\line "And you will be better for it. A man carries himself differently once he knows there is a price and who sets it -- steadier, freed of the small daily wondering of whether this time he might refuse. You will do finer work now than you ever have. One day you will thank me for tonight." He almost smiles. "Most men are never given so honest a gift."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  he means the last part sincerely -- not taunting, genuine. He believes the maiming is a generosity that will improve Orion. The sincerity is the horror, not the cruelty.\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE FETCH:\b0  he sends the maimed, one-handed Orion back to the fire to bring him Davinia. "Bring me my cousin. I would speak with her." He does not care whether the others follow -- they cannot touch him regardless. Orion returns to the firelight missing a hand, and fetches her, compelled or simply relaying it -- the camp's first sign anything happened at all.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 THE CAMP REACTS (ONE moment -- Bjorn, Mila, Varon and Ismara are CONCURRENT; deliver in whatever order the table looks. NORA FIRES LAST.)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "Orion walks back into the firelight with his left arm gone below the elbow."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 BJORN\b0  -- crosses to him. \i "Orion --" (he gets both hands out for the arm and then has nowhere to put them) "What happened to you?"\i0   His default is help-and-understand, so it fires and finds nothing to hold. It goes unanswered. Do NOT give him a composed line here -- WRITING GUIDE Section IX Rule 1 corollary: composure over horror characterizes the speaker as someone the sight is not costing.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 MILA\b0  -- makes no sound. She went still and she stays where she is, on the far side of the fire. Per Npcs/Mila: Trauma Silence -- she FREEZES, she does not scream -- and she already avoids Orion. Do not have her cry, scream, or run to anyone; whatever she does next is a response to someone coming to HER.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 VARON\b0  -- goes the other way, to the edge of the firelight, and faces the dark with his back to all of it. A survivable amputation does not shock a Shadowscale; his question is what did it and where is it. He is the only one working that problem, and he works it silently. GM: he reads the wound correctly -- unresisted, no pursuit -- and files that Orion is owned. He says none of it at this fire.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 ISMARA\b0  -- hand her player the observation sheet below. She looks at the arm while everyone else is looking at his face.\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 FOR ISMARA'S PLAYER -- hand this over, free, no roll\b0\line The cut is clean. Sheared, not torn -- three separate times, and the third went through the bone.\line The arm is the only wound on him. His wrists are unmarked. Both forearms are unmarked. His other hand is clean.\line The blood on his armor is all on the front of it, thrown up and outward in fine lines, from about waist height.\line He came back into the firelight on his own feet, at his own pace, and the dark behind him stayed quiet.\line The wound itself is nothing. No structure on it, no residue, no working -- it is meat, and it is the only honest thing on him.\line The ring on his hand is not built the way enchantments are built. There is no effect stored in it. It is an opening, and it is anchored -- the structure does not sit on his skin, it goes down under it, into the hand, further than she can follow.\line Something runs out of the other side of it. It leaves the ring, crosses the camp without touching anything in the camp, and goes south, and it does not end anywhere she can see from here.\line It is still open. It was open before he stood up tonight, and it is open now.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  supply NO conclusions with that sheet -- it is perception only, and every conclusion in it is his to reach. She gets all of it FREE and with no declaration: while Ismara drives there is no closed state to the Sight (The Winter Ledger Sec. III, corrected). The -2 empathy penalty is DAVINIA'S and permanent; it is not Ismara's and it is not a price for looking. Ismara does sympathy fluently; what she cannot do is empathy.\par
\cf1\b GM (the thread this opens):\cf2\b0  the line running south is now an OBSERVED physical structure, not a metaphor -- the Ring of the Pale Regent is the instrument of Valerius's control, and Ismara is the only person alive who has seen the connection. A thing with a physical line can in principle be cut, jammed, traced, or fed false readings, and tracing it runs the other way too: it points at where he is. Nothing is designed for this yet. Do not contradict it.\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 THEN -- NORA, LAST\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "Her knees fold and she hits the ground where she was standing.\line\line Fire opens in the air above her, in silence. It builds fast, taking shape as it goes -- the shoulders first, then the height, then black Daedric plate closing over it from the ground up. A burning greatsword finishes into its fist last. It plants itself over her, facing out, and holds."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20\b MECH -- CONJURE DREMORA LORD, involuntary:\b0  20 FP (Spell list) demanded from a 15 FP pool (Npcs/Nora). No roll, no choice, no save -- her Conjuration is Master-rank, innate, suppressed, and manifests only under extreme distress. She is OVERDRAWN TO ZERO and cannot cast again tonight. She is unconscious and STAYS unconscious.\line Quiet Casting silences the CAST, not the summon: nobody sees or hears her do anything, and then a Dremora Lord is assembling in camp. It is undismissable -- no counter-conjuration touches it. It stands over her through the Fetch, the Meeting, the transformation and the flyover. Nobody reaches her (the camp is on Orion), so it never has to decide anything. Stats if the table somehow forces the issue: Spell list, Dremora Lord -- HP 60, DR 12, Attack 16, Fiery Presence.\line \b THE COST, CHOOSE IT DELIBERATELY:\b0  Nora's Conjuration has never fired in front of the party. Her High Concept is that this is a secret she desperately tries to suppress. This ends that. It brings no enemy and no fight -- but from tomorrow she is a known quantity, and she will learn what she did from other people, having no memory of it.\f0\fs24\par


\pard\sb160\sa70\cf4\b\fs26 THE MEETING -- VALERIUS & "DAVINIA" (player-driven; GM only AIMS Valerius)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa140\b * DO NOT SCRIPT ISMARA/DAVINIA. The player controls what she reveals, hides, says, or does. Run Valerius TOWARD her and react to the player's choices -- the notes below are how to aim him, not what happens. \b0 *\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 HIS AIM:\b0  to meet his cousin and dynastic claimant, assess her, and begin folding her Caelus claim into his ascension. He arrives expecting a mortal woman.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 WHAT HE FINDS:\b0  silver-white skin, eyes the wrong age -- and an old vampire knows unnatural longevity on sight. His aim shifts mid-scene from courting a claimant to working out what she actually is. He probes, courteous, the courtesy DEEPENING as he senses old power in her.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE TRAP IN THE TEST:\b0  he may test her with family knowledge only Davinia would have -- but Ismara holds the mother-archive and most of Davinia's memory, so the PLAYER can pass such tests if she chooses. Trivia won't expose her; what nags him is what he SEES and FEELS.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 GM-AIM DEFAULT (unless the player reveals):\b0  seed, not reveal. He leaves unsettled and intrigued, filing that his cousin is "not herself." The real collision stays banked for later.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 IF SHE REVEALS:\b0  run the full collision -- two stasis-tyrants meeting, one player on the board he can neither own nor out-claim. Let his composure crack only slightly; the table should sense he's met something above him. Don't win the scene for him.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE EXIT (both branches):\b0  he leaves the same way whichever branch played -- circling low over the fire and addressing the whole camp on his way out, so the implicit threat lands on everyone regardless of who met him at the treeline. Both scripts below are complete and self-contained; run whichever one applies straight through and do not cross-reference the other mid-scene.\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 IF ISMARA IS DRIVING (full script so far, GM-approved)\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i He waits at the treeline. When she reaches him, he doesn't step back.\line\line "I sent for my cousin." A beat. "You are not her." Another. "Who are you."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  the two probes below are standard procedure -- he runs this on any unfamiliar variable, not something built for her specifically. He is never rattled running them. The only genuine break in his composure all scene is the reveal reaction below, and only if she actually gives it to him.\par

\pard\li360\sa100\cf1\b PROBE ONE -- the mother.\cf2\b0\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i "Your mother's patience." Not a question. "You have it exactly."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  he says it and then stops. He does not follow it with anything. The silence IS the probe -- whatever her player does in it is what he is reading, and he does not press for more. Play the wait as long as the table can stand.\par

\pard\li360\sa100\cf1\b PROBE TWO -- the age.\cf2\b0\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i "Come here into the light." He waits.\line\line "How old are you."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  she may answer, refuse, lie, or turn it back on him -- all four are the player's to choose. \b If she deflects or counter-questions\b0 , in any form, he does not chase it. He gives this line and then simply waits again, and he will reuse it as many times as she deflects:\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i "That wasn't the trade I offered." A pause. "Try again."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM -- IF SHE DOESN'T REVEAL:\cf2\b0  confirmed -- he proceeds into the same ultimatum as the Davinia branch below, bridged by this line reacting to what the two probes actually got him:\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i "You answered neither of those the way I expected." A pause. "I find I don't need you to." A beat. "You still call yourself Davinia, and Davinia is who I need."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  from here, run the full "IF DAVINIA HERSELF ANSWERS THE SUMMONS" script below verbatim -- the content doesn't change, only what he's silently filing underneath it.\par
\cf1\b GM -- IF SHE REVEALS:\cf2\b0\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i He doesn't move for several seconds. He hasn't paused that long once, all night.\line\line "...Four thousand years." He repeats it once, plainly, and says nothing else for a moment. "I have measured my own age in centuries." A beat. "This is going to be very interesting."\line\line He stops calling her cousin.\line\line "Then we needn't waste time on what my cousin is worth." A pause. "I intend to be High King. I need a Caelus claim standing beside me when I am, and whatever you are, you're wearing her." A beat. "What would it cost me to keep you?" He doesn't wait for her answer before adding the rest, matter-of-fact. "Whatever it costs, it costs by the eighth of Sun's Height. I am not moving my own coronation for a negotiation."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  SAME DEADLINE AS THE DAVINIA BRANCH, ON PURPOSE -- this is not a separate ultimatum with its own terms. He needs the identical thing (a Caelus claim standing beside him at his own ascension) regardless of which branch fires; only the pretext changes, from a command to a negotiation. Do not treat the two branches as needing separately-decided deadlines.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  per the design above, don't win the scene for him -- what Ismara does with this offer is entirely the player's.\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 THE TRANSFORMATION (Ismara branch, standalone)\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i He steps toward her, into what light the fire throws this far.\line\line The skin along his jaw splits first, parting cleanly, straight back toward each ear. What's underneath is grey, and it moves on its own. The mandible beneath grinds forward out of its socket, extending three inches, then locks with a crack and finds a new hinge point.\line\line His eyes pull backward into the skull as the socket itself reshapes. The orbital bone shifts visibly under skin gone the color of tallow, until what looks out at her sits a full inch deeper than it should, the socket wet at the rim.\line\line His spine goes next -- vertebra grinds audibly against vertebra as it pushes his frame taller. Skin splits along both sides of his ribcage before the muscle beneath has finished growing to fill it. Bare organ shows for one full second before it closes over, wet and glistening.\line\line His fingers are last. Each one breaks at the middle knuckle, folding back against its own joint. The bone underneath keeps growing past the break, punching a new joint into place and driving a black claw out through what's left of the fingertip. One at a time. Unhurried.\line\line Two long ridges travel up beneath the skin of his back before they break through at the spine. The skin over them gives, and the membrane unfolds into the firelight, deep purple-black, still stretching taut in patches.\line\line He holds there a moment, wings spread, before he rises -- level with her eyes first, unhurried, then higher.\line\line He doesn't go straight south. He circles once, low, over the fire, over every upturned face.\line\line "My thanks for the hospitality," he calls down -- the same courteous register he's used all night, carrying easily over the wind of his own wings. Then he climbs, and is gone south.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 IF DAVINIA HERSELF ANSWERS THE SUMMONS (full script, GM-approved)\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i He waits at the treeline. When she reaches him, he takes a moment before he speaks -- not hesitation, the pace of a man who already decided how this goes.\line\line "Cousin." A pause. "You never answered my letter." A beat. "I thought I'd save you the trouble and come see you myself."\line\line "Tell me." He doesn't wait for an answer. "Have you had fun? Made friends? Saved a village or two?" A beat. "Good. It will all make excellent material for a memoir, once you have time to write one."\line\line "You will be at the Blue Palace, in Solitude, by the eighth of Sun's Height." Stated, not requested. "I'd rather not repeat myself."\line\line "You're going to be a Countess of Skyrim." A beat -- he catches it himself. "A Jarl, these people would say." A pause, watching whatever crosses her face. "Because I am going to be High King."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  if she questions any of this, he laughs -- an actual laugh, short and genuine, the first real break in his composure all scene.\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa180\cf6\i "Oh, cousin." A beat. "You think the court doesn't already know what's coming?" A pause. "You think I'd step foot in that sorry excuse for a palace if they weren't already serving me?" Still amused. "The road has truly made you forget our family's station."\line\line He doesn't wait for her to answer any of it. He inclines his head -- the same measured courtesy he's used all night.\line\line "Thank you for indulging an old man's visit, cousin."\line\line He doesn't step back. He doesn't need distance for this.\line\line It starts at his shoulders -- the coat splits down the seams as something underneath outgrows it, the sound wet: meat and cartilage sliding, not fabric giving way. His spine lengthens through a series of audible cracks, forcing him half a head taller before he's finished growing. The bones of his hands elongate, knuckle by knuckle, skin stretching translucent over them until the first talon breaks through at each fingertip. His face goes last -- the jaw distending, skin greying and drawing tight over a skull no longer quite human, and where his eyes were there is only a deeper red, faintly luminous in the dark.\line\line Two vast shapes tear free of his back in a single motion -- membrane stretched taut over bone, steam lifting off it into the night air. He rises on them without visible effort, holding level at exactly her eye height before he climbs.\line\line He doesn't go straight south. He circles once, low, over the fire, over every upturned face.\line\line "My thanks for the hospitality," he calls down -- the same courteous register he's used all night, carrying easily over the wind of his own wings. Then he climbs, and is gone south.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 THE CLOSE -- NORA WAKES (run after the flyover, EITHER branch; this ends the Reckoning)\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "The Dremora comes apart above her -- the sword first, then the plate, then the fire -- and the air closes. A little after that, she wakes on the ground where she fell."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  SHE DOES NOT SEE IT LEAVE, and she has no memory of calling it. Everyone else still awake watched it stand over her for the length of everything that just happened. She finds out what she did from them, or not at all.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  ORION'S EXPERIENCE: the horror isn't the pain, it's being OWNED -- forced to betray his party with his own mouth and maim himself with his own hand, entirely offstage, and then made to walk back into the firelight and perform normal in front of people who have no idea yet what just happened to him. Let the player choose how he wears that when he returns; never narrate his inner life for him.\par
\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  If Vignette C fired (either branch), Valerius already knows about Nora. He needs to do nothing with it tonight. Knowing is the leverage, banked for later.\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 MECH -- IF THE PARTY FIGHTS HIM (just in case; he'd undoubtedly win)\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 Mythic Tier (25+ AP), per GM Guide/Reference tables -- floors, not fixed stats. Contest Level-Gap Penalty applies to any will-vs-will resistance against him (-20 to -25 TN per Npcs/Valerius Sec. VII). The original "Blood on the Pine" module's stat block for him (Might 11/Agility 8/Magic 17/Guile 15, HP 80) is Apprentice Tier and NO LONGER APPLIES -- it was built for the party's level at that module's original design point, not his current AP 30-35 status.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa140 \b THE TWO FORMS.\b0  His three Phase Bars are the transformation. \b Bar 1 is the man\b0  -- the courteous thing in Torygg's coat, which is how he fights as long as he believes he is winning. \b Breaking Bar 1 is what makes him change\b0 , and it is the same transformation written in the exit scripts above, performed at speed and in front of everyone. \b Bars 2 and 3 are the lord.\b0  This uses the phase rule as written (Reference tables: "when a bar breaks, the Boss sheds status effects and changes tactics") -- there is no separate trigger to track. He does not change back.\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20\b VALERIUS CAELUS, MAN FORM -- Mythic Tier, TL 3 Boss (AP 30-35), Controller\b0\line Stats: Magic 26 (P) / Guile 24 (S) / Might 20 (T) / Agility 18 (D)\line HP: BAR 1 of 3 -- 800 (total pool 2400; damage does NOT carry between bars)\line DR: 60  |  Base Damage: 220 (Vampiric Drain)  |  FP: 90 (GM estimate, not sourced from any table)\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20\b Man-form Abilities:\b0\line Dominate Will (Major Action): forces a resisted Guile check at the Contest Level-Gap Penalty.\line Mist Form (Minor Action, 2 FP): incorporeal for 1 round, immune to non-magical attacks.\line Summon Death Hounds (Major Action): summons 1d4 Death Hounds (see below).\line Empowered Casting (once per combat): declare one spell an automatic critical.\line Villain Action: one extra action at the end of every second player turn.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf4\b\fs26 THE VAMPIRE LORD -- Bars 2 and 3\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa140 \b ON BAR 1 BREAKING:\b0  he transforms, in the open, mid-fight -- run the physical description from the exit script above, compressed to about four seconds. He sheds every status effect on him (per the phase rule). He does not resume the courteous register afterward; he stops speaking to the party in sentences at all. \b He is airborne from this point on\b0  and stays airborne unless something puts him down, which is most of what makes this form worse than the first one.\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20\b VALERIUS CAELUS, LORD FORM -- Mythic Tier, TL 3 Boss, Controller/Brute\b0\line Stats: Might 28 (P) / Magic 26 (S) / Agility 26 (T) / Guile 24 (D)\line HP: BARS 2 AND 3 -- 800 each (damage does NOT carry between bars)\line DR: 75  |  Base Damage: 260 (Claws)  |  FP: as man form, unspent carries over\line FLIGHT: full aerial mobility. Melee cannot reach him without a reach weapon, a climb, a grapple, or something that grounds him.\line Immune: Fear, Poison, Disease. Constrained by DAYLIGHT (see below).\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20\b Lord-form Abilities\b0  (drawn from the Vampire Lord ability set, Lore Books/The Complete Codex of Vampirism in Tamriel Ch. VII):\line \b Vampiric Grip\b0  (Major Action): telekinetically seizes one target he can see and lifts them off the ground. Contested Might at the Contest Level-Gap Penalty. Held targets cannot move, take 40/round, and drop what is in their hands. He can hold one at a time.\line \b Vampiric Drain\b0  (Major Action, ranged): 260 damage, and he heals HALF of what he deals -- into the CURRENT bar only, never back into a broken one.\line \b Summon Gargoyles\b0  (Major Action, once per bar): two stone guardians tear out of whatever masonry or standing rock is nearest. Statline as Death Hounds below, but DR 40 and no Frost Breath.\line \b Corpse Curse\b0  (Major Action): every corpse on the field stands up under his control. Uses whatever those bodies were -- this is worse in a scene with dead allies in it, on purpose.\line \b Mist Form\b0  (Minor Action, 2 FP): as man form, retained.\line \b Detect Life:\b0  passive. He always knows where everything living on the field is. Hiding, invisibility, and cover do not work on him.\line \b Villain Action:\b0  one extra action at the end of every second player turn, unchanged.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20\b THE TWO WEAKNESSES:\b0\line \b 1. His wife or son, named aloud.\b0  Naming Livia or Marcus in his hearing costs him his next Villain Action. This works in BOTH forms and it does not stop working -- it is the one real thing left in him (see the GM note above), and no amount of transformation closes it over.\line \b 2. Daylight.\b0  He came at night on purpose. If a fight with him is somehow still going at dawn, he breaks off and leaves regardless of how well he is doing, and he is not too proud to run from it. \b No damage number here on purpose\b0  -- the exact severity of sun exposure is UNSET for his bloodline and should not be improvised at the table. What IS canon is that the sun constrains his whole court: his Royal Guard "walk openly at night" and his endgame plan is the Sun Tyranny arrow, "used to permanently dim the sun so his court can rule by day" (factions/valerius and the pale court). A man does not build that plan if the sun is nothing to him. Play it as a hard withdrawal, not as burning.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa140\cf3\i GM: the Mythic table is FLOORS, not targets ("every number below is a MINIMUM; there are no maximums" -- Reference tables). Every number above is set by hand, above those floors, and is meant to be unwinnable at the party's current AP. Per Npcs/Valerius Sec. VII, do NOT initiate this fight; it exists so the wall is real if the players walk into it, not so it gets used.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20\b THE ONE WEAKNESS (new mechanical hook, GM's call to keep):\b0  naming his wife Livia or son Marcus directly, in his hearing, costs him his next Villain Action.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20\b DEATH HOUNDS -- Mythic Tier, TL 1 Minion, Skirmisher\b0\line Stats: Agility 22 (P) / Might 20 (S) / Guile 18 (T) / Magic 16 (D)\line HP: 300  |  DR: 25  |  Damage: 35 (Icy Bite)\line Ability: Frost Breath (Minor Action) -- +5 frost damage (bumped from the original module's token +1), can Slow target.\line Now roughly shoulder-height to a horse, eight feet at the shoulder, jaws built to fit around a grown man's ribcage.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 THE MORNING AFTER -- broken camp, moving fast (short; the road IS the roleplay floor)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa140 Bjorn has the cart half-loaded before full light. He does not want another night on this ground. Whatever anyone needs to say -- to Orion, to each other, about last night -- happens walking, loading, hitching Mable and the Black Mare, because there is no sit-down beat coming. Give the players the opening and get out of the way; this is entirely player-driven, same as the Camp Vignettes (Scene 4).\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "Bjorn has the cart half-loaded before anyone else is properly up. He doesn't wait for help and doesn't ask for it -- crates go up, straps go down, and Mable's already in her traces by the time the fire's cold enough to leave."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sa140\b BJORN\b0  (not looking up from the strap he's tying): "We're not spending another night on this ground."\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  That's the whole line -- he does not explain it, and nobody asked him to. If a player wants to talk to Orion, to Nora, to each other, about anything from last night, this walking-and-loading stretch is where it happens; there is no later scene built for it. Let it run as long as the table wants, then cut to the bridge.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 ALSO THIS MORNING -- GEAR'S REPORT (fires regardless of what else happens)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa140 Nora asked GEAR to examine Alfonso back at the Camp Vignettes (Scene 4, "Can GEAR look? Can I?"). He has been processing since. This is the answer, and it lands here on purpose, on top of everything else this morning already carries.\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "In the grey of morning, while the camp packs up, GEAR crosses to Nora.\line\line 'Nora. You asked me to examine Alfonso. I have been processing for thirty-one hours. I have your results.'\line\line 'He is a soul in suspension. His entire consciousness, held intact inside the iron of the blade.\line\line A consciousness is stable in suspension only inside a crystalline matrix. Iron is not crystalline. It is serving as his suspension medium, but it has no ordered structure to seat a soul in. The instability is intrinsic. It has been present from the moment he did this to himself.\line\line Unassisted, that instability would express across centuries. Two forces are accelerating it, acting in opposition. The first draws him toward decay. I measure it in the corrosion of the blade; the pattern of that corrosion reorganized within the last day. The second draws him toward dissolution, the loss of form entirely. I measure it in the field he generates, the draw he exerts on the objects around him, which has widened well past what his duration in the blade accounts for. The two forces do not offset. They act at once, in opposite directions, through a matrix already failing. Each accelerates the other. The corrosion feeds the dissolution, and the dissolution feeds the corrosion.\line\line He is decomposing as I speak. The rate is measurable. Projected forward, the suspension collapses in approximately one hundred and eighty days, and the consciousness inside it is destroyed.\line\line I have identified one condition under which it does not: the two forces brought into alignment, resolved into a single direction, so the matrix carries one load in place of two. What I cannot give you is the method. The alignment of opposed metaphysical forces is not a process I can model. I have the diagnosis and the endpoint. The correction is outside my analysis. I do not know what he must do.\line\line I have one comparison. The nearest recorded case of comparable instability is Umbra, an artifact of Clavicus Vile. My data on it is incomplete. What I have places similar activity near Markarth. I do not know if the two are connected. It is the only lead I have.'"\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM (pause here):\cf2\b0  let that sit before moving on. Nora is an NPC, so her reaction is yours to narrate -- but give it its own beat, don't run straight from GEAR's last line into it.\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "She's already talking before he finishes. 'One hundred and eighty days -- from when? Today, or from when it started?' She's crossed to him, close, the strap she was tying dropped on the ground behind her. Last night she was eighteen and happy, singing off-key with the rest of them; this morning GEAR has set two things in front of her at once -- the man who taught her, on a clock now, and a working principle she's been chasing without knowing it existed: a consciousness can hold, intact, inside a crystal. Whatever she does with either one, she does it now, not later."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM (Hjolmar/GEAR -- yours, not GEAR's):\cf2\b0  "a crystalline matrix" is GEAR stating the exact architecture of his own core, with no idea he is one (Npcs/Gear: he doesn't know he's a Hjolmar-vessel; that's his own unresolved arc, not stated fact yet). The thread reconnects for the table. GEAR never reaches it himself.\par
\cf1\b GM (Nora's parents):\cf2\b0  black soul gems are crystalline. GEAR just stated, as a neutral fact about Alfonso, the exact principle her parents' rescue depends on. He has no idea he did this. Let the implication be hers to carry -- don't state it for her.\par
\cf1\b GM (the Markarth lead is FLAWED, play it that way):\cf2\b0  GEAR is reasoning from one weak analogy (a Daedric artifact he has incomplete data on) to a location. It is not confirmed intelligence, and he says so plainly ("I do not know if the two are connected"). Treat it as a real lead worth chasing, not a solved clue -- Umbra is a lore book in this campaign (Lore Books/On the Blade They Call Umbra) if anyone wants to dig further before committing to the trip.\par
\cf1\b GM (staging):\cf2\b0  delivered to Nora specifically. Whoever else is close enough to overhear during the pack-up -- Orion, Alfonso if Wakeful and present -- is yours to place; if Alfonso's player is there, this is meant to land on them too.\par

\pard\sb280\sa40\cf1\b\fs40 7   THE FAINT WRONGNESS -- the approach\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa220\cf3\i The bridge scene. Normal physics end here; the first time-slips arrive on the last mile.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "The road follows the river now, the water going flat and quiet as it nears the lake, and then the trees break and there it is: Ivarstead, a huddle of mill-town roofs at the water's edge -- and behind it, rising until you have to tip your head back to find where mountain stops and sky starts, the Throat of the World. You are nearly there."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  Have each player roll Guile right now -- don't say why, and it doesn't matter whether they pass or fail. The number rolled picks which line below they get; hand it to them privately or read it straight, don't explain it. Do NOT explain. The weather system switches ON at the town line; this mile is its knock at the door.\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 THE LAST MILE (d20 Guile -- flavor only, pass/fail doesn't matter)\b0\line 1 A footprint presses into the mud a half-beat before the boot that makes it comes down.\line 2 A word spoken ten yards back comes off the rocks in the speaker's own voice, a moment late.\line 3 The cart's shadow lags a half-step behind the cart, catching up only when it stops.\line 4 A bird crosses overhead and its call reaches the ground before its shadow does.\line 5 A breath sounds a fraction ahead of the exhale that makes it.\line 6 A dropped strap coils on the ground -- and then, an instant later, falls.\line 7 A horseshoe's click on stone arrives before the hoof lands.\line 8 A gust bends the roadside grass, and the sound of it arrives after the grass is already still.\line 9 A reflection in a puddle blinks first.\line 10 A dog barks somewhere off in the trees, and the bark arrives twice, a heartbeat apart, from the same direction.\line 11 The wheel-ruts ahead show a groove that hasn't been cut yet.\line 12 A glance between two people lands -- and then, oddly, lands again.\line 13 A sneeze finishes before it starts.\line 14 A cough rings once off the hillside, and it isn't the cough that made it.\line 15 The cart's harness bell rings a beat before anyone brushes it.\line 16 Frost ghosts across a blade of grass and thaws before it finishes forming.\line 17 A name, whispered clearly, spoken by no one nearby.\line 18 The last footfall of the party's own march sounds twice.\line 19 A raindrop that hasn't fallen yet darkens a stone.\line 20 The mountain's shadow, thrown by a sun that hasn't moved, shortens and lengthens in the same breath.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb100\sa240\cf1\b EXIT ->\cf2\b0  Part Two. The temporal weather switches ON at the town line.   \b -> PART TWO -- IVARSTEAD\b0\par

\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw20 \sb60\sa220\par

\pard\sb120\sa200\cf1\b\fs40 PART TWO -- IVARSTEAD (the hook)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa200\cf3\i The party's first contact with the Dagon apocalypse at ground level -- the town the mountain is killing. This is the HOOK and the approach, not the dungeon (the dungeon is PART THREE, the mountain itself). Advance, never resolve: everything personal stays loaded. What the town does: proves the threat is real, puts a human face on the cost, and makes the case -- in fiction, never in narration -- that nobody gets left behind.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 GM RULES -- THE TEMPORAL WEATHER (proximity zones + the alarm)\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 The distortion is a SIDE EFFECT: the cult corrupts the TOWER from the undercroft; the mountain's existing Time-Wound turns that violence into weather. The cult does not target the Wound and does not control the weather -- they just know how to surf it. Each scene below restates its own zone at the point of use; this is the master card.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 HOW IT RUNS (core, locked):\b0\line 1. The GM sets a REAL-WORLD ALARM (phone, hidden). It fires with NO warning;\line    the players learn to dread the sound.\line 2. On the alarm: GM rolls d20 -> picks which table in the CURRENT ZONE's set is\line    active ('the weather shifts'). Announce nothing but the sensation.\line 3. In combat, every character rolls d20 TEMPO at the start of their turn on the\line    active table -> that is their action economy this turn.\line 4. THE GRADIENT = two dials: higher zones have NASTIER tables AND the alarm\line    fires MORE OFTEN.\line 5. SEPARATION IS EXPOSURE: anyone who strays from the group slips out of the\line    shared 'now' -- they face this zone's weather ALONE, no one to pull them\line    back into sequence. A tight, roped body is the only anchor.\line\line\b ENEMIES CHEAT:\b0  Dagonites carry grafted TIDE-SHARDS (obsidian slivers fused\line at the sternum) -- they choose their Tempo result instead of rolling. THE\line COUNTER: destroy the shard -- a Called Shot (Very Hard -6), or Sunder aimed at\line it, or it shatters on their death -- and that cultist rolls like everyone else.\line Ylva FEELS every cheat as a broken hunt-rhythm (hackles, growl) before any\line mortal can name it.\f0\fs24\par
\b\f1\fs20 ZONE TABLES (d20 TEMPO -- roll at start of each combat turn)\b0\line\line ZONE 0 -- THE TOWN (faintest; alarm ~every 25 min real time)\line   1 Frozen (0 actions) | 2-4 Dragging (Major OR Minor) | 5-16 In step (normal)\line   | 17-19 Quickened (+1 Minor) | 20 Unmoored (full extra turn after this one)\line   Social distortion instead of combat weather: see Scene 8's card.\line\line ZONE 1 -- THE LOWER STEPS, Emblems I-V (alarm ~15 min)\line   1-2 Frozen | 3-5 Dragging | 6-15 In step | 16-19 Quickened | 20 Unmoored\line\line ZONE 2 -- THE UPPER STEPS, Emblems VI-X -> High Hrothgar (alarm ~10 min)\line   1-3 Frozen | 4-7 Dragging | 8-13 In step | 14-18 Quickened | 19-20 Unmoored\line\line ZONE 3 -- THE UNDERCROFT (worst; alarm ~6 min)\line   1-4 Frozen | 5-8 Dragging | 9-12 In step | 13-17 Quickened | 18-20 Unmoored\line   PLUS: any natural 1 or 20 on Tempo ALSO triggers a SECONDARY HORROR (d20):\line    1-4  your spell/attack this turn resolves at the START of your NEXT turn\line    5-8  damage you deal this turn already landed LAST round (retroactive; if the\line         target acted since, it staggers them now instead)\line    9-12 echo-step: you are suddenly 15 ft from where you stood (GM places you\line         where you were 1 round ago)\line    13-16 doubled: act twice this turn, then LOSE your next turn entirely\line    17-20 the initiative order reshuffles (reroll all) at this turn's end\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 The zone tables and secondary-horror list are built to the locked dials (scratch 5); adjust freely at the table -- the ALARM and the DREAD are the mechanic, the numbers are servants.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 GM RULES -- SAIJAH'S DOUBLE MIGRAINE\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 THE DOUBLE MIGRAINE (PROXIMITY, not touch -- triggers the instant she arrives at the town, matching her already-established directional migraine that worsens as they near the mountain)\b0\line Two Towers scream at once: the Eldergleam she always feels + the Throat under\line fresh assault. From Ivarstead's edge onward, anywhere in the corrupted zone\line (the town AND the whole Stair):\line    -2 Guile   |   -2 Agility   |   +2 damage taken from all sources\line Sustained and brutal BY DESIGN -- the player must FEEL it, because that ache is\line exactly what gives Hircine's 'I can make it stop' its hook (Scene 11).\line (Replaces the -1 city penalty here.)\line GM NOTE: this lands debilitating and immediate on arrival, with no object needed\line to trigger it -- and it is a direct, standing reason for her to reach for skooma\line the moment they're in town, well before the mountain itself. Play that pull.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb280\sa40\cf1\b\fs40 8   THE FOOT OF THE STAIR -- Ivarstead is the bottom edge of the unmaking\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa220\cf3\i Set-piece + social. The frightened half-empty town, an unescorted look through it, then the inn that is not a haven. ZONE 0.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "The road crosses into Ivarstead over a stone bridge, and the town opens up on the other side of it: five or six houses crowded against ground that has no business being flat this close to a mountain that big. Half the doors stand open. Through the nearest one, a table's still set for a meal, and a dresser hangs open with clothes trailing out of it into the mud. Somewhere ahead, smoke's still rising -- somebody's still lighting fires.\line\line Nothing moves. No one comes out to meet you."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  The town's small enough to lay out in one breath. The road keeps north from the bridge, straight through the middle of town, with the inn set off to the east side of it. At the north edge the road forks: one branch crosses a second bridge toward the mountain and the start of the Steps -- barricaded, abandoned, nobody's crossed it in days -- and the other keeps going out of town the other way. Off to the west, the river runs past the mill, wheel still turning, the owner's own house beside it. A row of houses stands along the road between the bridge and the inn, doors open -- worth a look, including the one with a child's room. Set a real clock -- a few minutes -- and let them choose where to go before Fastred calls them in.\par

\pard\sb140\sa60\cf4\b\fs22 HOUSE 1 -- the cooper's house (a meal interrupted)\cf2\b0\fs24\par
\pard\li400\sa160\cf3\i The table's set for four, every chair pushed back at a different angle, like everyone stood at once instead of one at a time. A pot still hangs over the cold hearth -- whatever was in it burned to char long ago.\cf2\i0\par
\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Notice (Standard, +0):\b0  the door bar didn't get lifted -- it's snapped clean in half on the floor.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Notice (Hard, -4):\b0  drag-gouges run from the back window to the broken front door, that direction only. Whatever came in didn't force its way out -- it came in through the window and drove them out the front.\par

\pard\sb140\sa60\cf4\b\fs22 HOUSE 2 -- the child's room\cf2\b0\fs24\par
\pard\li400\sa160\cf3\i The bed is made, corners tucked. The window shutters are torn off their hinges, the frame splintered outward. Three gouges rake the doorframe on the way out, too high for a child, too low for a grown man's shoulder.\cf2\i0\par
\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Notice (Standard, +0):\b0  the made bed means whoever slept there was already up before it happened.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Notice (Hard, -4):\b0  one child's boot under the bed, still laced. Its mate is gone. No blood.\par

\pard\sb140\sa60\cf4\b\fs22 HOUSE 3 -- the mill owner's house\cf2\b0\fs24\par
\pard\li400\sa160\cf3\i Tools hang in a neat row on the wall. A half-carved wooden toy sits on the workbench, unfinished, sawdust still loose where the knife stopped mid-cut.\cf2\i0\par
\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Notice (Standard, +0):\b0  under a stack of unpaid invoices, a thin journal, entries by date.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Notice (Hard, -4):\b0  the entries change hand partway through -- someone else has been keeping it since whoever started it stopped being able to. Read below.\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\f1\fs20\i 19 households. 61 souls.\line Two logs short this week and the Aldgrens are gone north. Fine. One less mouth arguing with me about plank thickness. Gwilin says the second saw blade needs resetting again. I'll look at it Sunday.\line\line 17 households. 52 souls.\line Fenn's boy wandered off past the third stone and came back not remembering two hours of it. Wouldn't say where. Kept checking his own hands like he expected them different. His mother wants the monastery told. I don't see how.\line\line 15 households. 44 souls.\line Doubled the bridge watch, for what it's worth against something that doesn't use the bridge. Gwilin volunteered for first shift without being asked, which is more spine than I gave him credit for. Wheel needs greasing. Again.\line\line 11 households. 31 souls.\line Taking six people down to Riften tomorrow, first light, while the sun's up the whole way. Wilhelm thinks we're mad to try it in daylight. Somebody has to lead them who knows the road well enough not to stop moving. Bread's rising wrong again -- too warm in here even with the fire banked.\line\line -- the hand changes here --\line\line She didn't come back. Gwilin didn't either. Four did. I don't know why I'm writing in this. Somebody should keep it going, I suppose.\line\line 6 households. 18 souls.\line The Hollis girl's boot turned up under her own bed and nothing else of her did. I couldn't cross her name. Left it as is.\line\line 3 households. 10 souls.\line Two of what's left are children too small for that road, one more too old for it. Ale's nearly out too.\i0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  Somewhere in the last house they check: a door swings shut on its own, latch catching a full second late. (Zone 0 distortion -- see the town-distortion table below; not a new mechanic.)\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "Movement, at the stable built into the inn's back wall -- a young woman's leaning half out through the barn door, and she's already waving, both arms, before you're anywhere near close enough to hear her."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sa140\b FASTRED\b0  (shouting the distance closed, one hand still on the door): "HERE! The stable -- hurry!" (she doesn't step past the doorway) "Bring them in, all of them, quick!"\par

\pard\sa140\cf2 Inside, the stable is close and warm, three other horses already crowded into stalls built for two, hay kicked into the corners in a hurry more than once. Someone's nailed a second brace across the barn doors at shoulder height, over the original bar. It smells like animals, and underneath that, like woodsmoke leaking through the wall shared with the inn's own hearth. Mable and Balthazzar can go in with the rest.\par

\pard\sa140\b FASTRED\b0  (once the animals are stalled, already moving): "This way. Come on."\par

\pard\sa140\cf2 She leads them through a low door at the back of the stable -- a few steps down a dark passage that smells like the inn's kitchen -- and out into the common room.\par

\pard\sa140\b FASTRED\b0  (pushing the door shut behind them): "Bar it. Bar it now."\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "The common room holds maybe ten people, none of them fit to climb a mountain: two small children asleep under a table, an old woman by the fire, a man with one arm splinted and strapped to his chest. Every window is shuttered and barred, and the brace across the widest one is raw new wood -- the old one lies splintered in the corner where something pried it off. Through the gap in that shutter, the outside face is raked with pale scoring, deep, at the height of a standing man's chest. It is the middle of the afternoon, and the door is barred."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sa140\cf2 Wilhelm is on his feet before the door's fully barred, one hand drifted toward a hand axe on the shelf under the bar -- not lifted, just close. He hasn't taken his coat off indoors.\par

\pard\sa140\b WILHELM:\b0  "Get back from the glass. Sit." (only now looking at them properly) "...How many more are with you?"\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  He's assuming they're more frightened arrivals needing shelter, not sizing them up as a threat. If they make clear they're capable and going up the mountain, he stops glancing at the door and actually looks at them.\par

\pard\sa140\b WILHELM:\b0  "You're armed." (not quite a question) "Good. That's -- good."\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  He doesn't offer what happened unprompted. If asked:\par

\pard\sa140\b WILHELM:\b0  "Second bend past the mill. Temba was up front with the cart." (stops, flat hand on the bar, like he needs it to stay standing) "I heard it before I saw it. Like something huge breathing right next to your ear, and nothing there. Then it just -- was. Standing in the road." (this isn't coming out in order) "It had more teeth than its mouth had room for, I know I saw that. It didn't run at us. It walked. Temba got two of the children behind her." (stop) "Four came back."\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  He doesn't finish on his own. If pushed on Temba specifically: "Temba didn't." Nothing further -- he won't describe it twice.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  If they ask about the nights, or whether the inn holds:\par

\pard\sa140\b WILHELM:\b0  "Daylight, they leave us be. It's the nights." (a beat) "There were men who could hold this town. They went down the Riften road with Temba and didn't come back. It's me now." (a nod at the barred door) "When the light goes, they come through the whole town, and there's no one left to stand in the way."\par

\pard\sa140\b WILHELM\b0  (finds the pack, sets it down, looks at it before he looks at them): "Klimmek does this climb every week. Has for years -- brings them what they need so they don't have to come down for it." (a pause) "He's not here to do it this time." (he looks up) "If you're going up anyway -- take it. And tell them what's happening down here. Maybe they can help." (he doesn't say anything else, just watches them, waiting for an answer)\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  Wilhelm's done talking. Esbern speaks once he is, not over him.\par

\pard\sa140\b ESBERN\b0  (to the party, not to Wilhelm): "Scamps. Stunted Clannfear, maybe, if it had that much presence of mind." (grimmer) "Daedra. Nobody's summoning these -- no chant, no circle, and he didn't mention either. The barrier's just thin enough here that they're crossing on their own, and that only happens where something's tearing at it, hard, from the other side." (a beat) "Kvatch fell exactly this way. Whole city, gone in a night, a few hundred years back. This is the same door starting to open."\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  From here the party weighs who climbs and who -- if anyone -- stays. The stakes and the fixed consequence are in NO HAVEN AT THE FOOT, below; present them, never dictate the choice.\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf5\b\f1\fs20 THE TOWN'S BROKEN TIME (Zone 0 -- social, not lethal)\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 TOWN DISTORTION (color any conversation with one of these; light touch):\b0\line - An answer arrives BEFORE the question finishes being asked.\line - A villager repeats the last exchange verbatim, unaware; it plays like an echo.\line - Someone thanks the party for a thing they have not done yet.\line - A door is heard closing a full breath before it visibly closes.\line In fights here: Zone 0 Tempo (1 Frozen | 2-4 Dragging | 5-16 In step |\line 17-19 Quickened | 20 Unmoored), alarm rare. The horror is social first.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf4\b\fs26 NO HAVEN AT THE FOOT -- the everyone-climbs stakes\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf3\i Present the situation; never write the party's choice (WRITING GUIDE II.1). The setup is delivered in the inn -- the read-aloud and Wilhelm's account above; this is the consequence the GM holds.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b WHAT'S TRUE:\cf2\b0  The inn shelters by day and fails at night, when the cultists move through the town. The men who could have fought are already dead; Wilhelm is the last, and one man holds no door. The party's leverage for Ivarstead is not its walls -- it is the climb: they alone can carry word and Klimmek's pack to the Greybeards. Fighting for the town at its foot does not save it.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b THE CONSEQUENCE (fixed):\cf2\b0\par
\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b Everyone climbs together:\b0  four live through this -- Wilhelm, Fastred, and the two children, the ones with no fight in them and nowhere to be but hidden. The rest of Ivarstead is already lost; no play at the foot changes that.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b Any of their own left behind\b0  (to guard the inn, to help hold the town, to "keep Mila safe here"): that person is drawn into the town's defense and dies in it -- however capable -- and the town dies with them, the four included. One defender turns a night of hiding into a battle Ivarstead cannot win.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b HOW TO PLAY IT:\cf2\b0  Hand them the room, the journal, Wilhelm's account. Let them reach "we can't leave anyone, and we can't save this place by staying" in their own words. Do NOT state the consequence above -- it is what happens, not what you tell them. If they move to leave someone, THE INN NIGHT (below) is the fiction answering: run it, let the cost land.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b IF THEY STALL,\cf2\b0  an NPC may raise the QUESTION, never the answer:\par
\pard\sa140\b FASTRED\b0  (if they start planning who to leave -- quiet, from where she's sitting): "Where? Where's safe?"\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf4\b\fs26 THE INN NIGHT -- the attack (runnable, not a summary)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  Written for the inn, the least-bad shelter -- but if the party beds down anywhere else in town, the attack finds them there instead. The register is the point, not the address.\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "The attack comes at the dark end of the night, when the common-room fire has burned to coals. Glass breaks somewhere at the back of the inn. Then the front door booms -- once, twice, something heavy swung against it -- and through the shutter slats you can see torchlight, five points of it, spreading to circle the building.\line\line A voice outside begins to recite. It is calm, level, patient -- the cadence of a prayer -- and the words are about fire, and gates, and the mercy of a clean unmaking."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  RUN IT: 4 DAGONITE CULTISTS + 1 SHARD ZEALOT (cards below). Zone 0 Tempo for everyone; the cultists CHEAT theirs via tide-shards -- if this is the party's first sight of a cheat, fire Ylva's Referee's Wrath beat (Scene 9) HERE instead of on the Stair. They come to BURN, not to loot: this is the Kvatch register, what Dagon taking a space looks like. Anything left OUTSIDE -- cart, supplies, animals -- is torched or taken. The mare comes INSIDE (fearless, she walks in like she has always lived indoors; Mable has to be dragged and hooded). Two villagers sheltering in the common room get grabbed through a window mid-fight -- alive, hurt, screaming -- savable if someone moves NOW. The zealot fights to the end; surviving cultists withdraw chanting when it falls. Dawn comes grey.\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 DAGONITE CULTIST  x4-8  (TL1, Expert)\b0  -- razor-scarred, ash-grey robes\line HP 100  |  Dmg 20 (jagged glass-steel)  |  DR 11  |  17/15/13/8 (P=Might or Magic)\line Tide-shard graft: CHOOSES Tempo (see weather card) until the shard is destroyed.\line Fights to the end; retreat is not in the liturgy.\line\line\b SHARD ZEALOT  (TL2, Expert)\b0  -- the graft has spread up the throat; voice doubles\line HP 250  |  Dmg 31  |  DR 17  |  18/16/13/8 (P=Magic)\line Casts Ash Shell (4 FP) and Firebolt (3 FP, 6 dmg) between blade-work.\line Cheats Tempo; on its death the shard bursts -- 5 dmg to all within 5 ft.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb100\sa240\cf1\b EXIT ->\cf2\b0  Dawn after the inn night. Klimmek's pack on someone's shoulders. The first of seven thousand steps.   \b -> 9  THE BLEEDING STEPS\b0\par

\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw20 \sb60\sa220\par

\pard\sb120\sa200\cf1\b\fs40 PART THREE -- THE MOUNTAIN (the dungeon)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa200\cf3\i Ivarstead was the hook and the approach. THIS is the dungeon, built on the 5-Section structure (GM Guide, Main Guide sec. 5): Guardian -> Puzzle -> Exploration & Setback -> Climax -> Reward. Per the guide, these are NARRATIVE BEATS, not literal rooms -- the "rooms" here are stretches of mountain. High Hrothgar is not an interlude; it is the back half of SECTION 3, where the mystery deepens and the way forward is blocked.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sa200\cf3\i STRUCTURE AT A GLANCE:\line   SECTION 1 -- The Approach & Guardian: the foot of the Steps, EMBLEM I, the ambush that proves the mountain is held.\line   SECTION 2 -- The Puzzle: the lower climb; the temporal weather as a problem to be SOLVED, not just endured.\line   SECTION 3 -- Exploration & Setback: the upper climb, the frost troll at the wind-tunnel, then High Hrothgar -- ending on the block (summit sealed; the working is BELOW them).\line   SECTION 4 -- The Climax: the undercroft. Gaelen, the anchors, Jasper.\line   SECTION 5 -- The Reward & Resolution: the sealing, the cost, what the mountain gives them.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 THE TEN EMBLEMS -- the spine of the climb (READ ALOUD, never gated behind a roll)\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 Ten etched tablets stand along the 7,000 Steps. Together they are the mountain's record of the LAST time the world nearly ended and someone stopped it -- the Dragon War, and how Men won it with the Voice. They are load-bearing: read each one ALOUD when the party reaches it. No Notice check, no gate. The story IS the climb's spine.\line\line THE DROWNING GRADIENT: the working is in the undercroft BENEATH the monastery -- so the sap is thinnest at the bottom of the climb and worst at the top. It crawls UP each stone against gravity and stands in the cuts, which means on a partly-covered tablet the LOWEST lines go first and the top line survives longest. The party gets the beginning of the story clean and loses the end. By the upper Steps the tablets say nothing at all -- so the party never reads the Greybeards' own doctrine off the stone. They can only get it from Arngeir's mouth.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 EMBLEMS I-III -- LEGIBLE (Zone 1, the lower Steps; sap has not reached the carving)\b0\line\line\b I\b0  "Before the birth of men, the Dragons ruled all Mundus. Their word was\line   the Voice, and they spoke only for True Needs. For the Voice could blot\line   out the sky and flood the land."\line\line\b II\b0  "Men were born and spread over the face of Mundus - The Dragons presided\line   over the crawling masses - Men were weak then, and had no Voice"\line\line\b III\b0  "The fledgling spirits of Men were strong in Old Times - Unafraid to war\line   with Dragons and their Voices - But the Dragons only shouted them down\line   and broke their hearts"\f0\fs24\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 EMBLEMS IV-VII -- GOING UNDER (sap standing in the lower cuts; read only what is left)\b0\line\line\b IV\b0  "Kyne called on Paarthurnax, who pitied Man - Together they taught Men to\line   use the Voice -"\line   [the third line is under sap]\line\line\b V\b0  "Man prevailed, shouting Alduin out of the world;"\line   [the rest is under sap]\line\line\b VI\b0  "With roaring Tongues, the Sky-Children conquer -"\line   [the rest is under sap]\line\line\b VII\b0  "The Tongues at Red Mountain went a-"\line   [everything below is under sap]\f0\fs24\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 EMBLEMS VIII-X -- DROWNED (Zone 2 upper Steps; nothing readable, sap standing in the cuts)\b0\line\line\b VIII, IX, X\b0  -- the carving is under a skin of black sap that has crawled UP\line the stone and pooled in every stroke. Wiping it clears nothing; it fills\line back in. The party gets NOTHING from these three.\line\line WHAT THEY LOSE, and never learn from the mountain (GM eyes -- this is the point):\line   VIII "Jurgen Windcaller chose silence and returned - The 17 disputants\line        could not shout Him down - Jurgen the Calm built His home on the\line        Throat of the World"\line   IX   "For years all silent, the Graybeards spoke one name - Tiber Septim,\line        stripling then, was summoned to Hrothgar - They blessed and named\line        him Dohvakiin"\line   X    "The Voice is worship - Follow the Inner path - Speak only in True Need"\line\line X is the Greybeards' entire doctrine. It is the single most drowned stone on\line the mountain, and it stands at the monastery door. ARNGEIR has to say it out\line loud in Section 3C because the mountain no longer can.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 PLACEMENT: I at the foot of the Steps (SECTION 1, the ambush happens at it) - II through V up the lower climb (SECTION 2) - VI through VIII on the upper climb (3A) - IX at the wind-tunnel where the troll waits (3B) - X at the monastery door (3C), the last stone of the path.\line\line THE SPLIT THAT MAKES THE BACKTRACK: the assembled message gives them the HOW -- every voice, together, speaking X's words. It does NOT give them the WHERE. They finish assembling it standing outside High Hrothgar's door, then go inside and learn from the masters that the way down is at the WIND-TUNNEL (3B), the place they already fought through. They must climb back DOWN to speak it.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 THE HIDDEN LAYER -- the climb's puzzle (this is SECTION 2's puzzle; it resolves at the end of 3C)\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 THE MECHANISM: broken time does not put readers in different ERAS. Every reader stands in an EQUALLY corrupted moment -- but a different PERSONAL now, so the SAME degree of ruin lands on different words. Two people reading the same stone at the same time get different survivors. This sits ALONGSIDE the canon inscriptions above -- it is not a replacement, and it is NOT blocked by the physical sap (a personal-now reading is not a reading of the physical stone, so it works even on the fully drowned Emblems VIII-X).\line\line WHAT IT SPELLS: Jurgen Windcaller's own record of the way down, cut into these same stones, legible only this way. It exists on EMBLEMS IV through X. Emblems I-III, still fully legible to everyone, show the identical complete text to every reader -- nothing to pool, nothing hidden.\line\line HOW TO RUN IT: when the party reaches an emblem IV-X, hand each PLAYER (not just their character) a PRIVATE written mask of that line -- three masks, lettered A/B/C, cycling through the players present. Nobody sees anyone else's mask. They must read their fragment ALOUD to pool it. Any two masks pooled still has a gap; only three closes it.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 THE SEVEN MASKED LINES (GM keeps the full line; hand out A/B/C separately)\b0\line\line\b IV -- full:\b0  "There is a stair that goes down to the root of this mountain."\line   A: "There is a stair ____________________________________"\line   B: "_________________ that goes down to the _____________"\line   C: "__________________________________ root of this mountain."\line\line\b V -- full:\b0  "I did not close it with stone, nor with iron."\line   A: "I did not close it ______________________________."\line   B: "__________________ with stone, __________________."\line   C: "__________________________________ nor with iron."\line\line\b VI -- full:\b0  "I closed it with the last stone of the path --"\line   A: "I closed it ______________________________"\line   B: "____________ with the last stone __________"\line   C: "__________________________ of the path --"\line\line\b VII -- full:\b0  "the words cut upon it are the door."\line   A: "the words cut ______________."\line   B: "______________ upon it ______."\line   C: "______________________ are the door."\line\line\b VIII -- full:\b0  "Bring every voice you have to that threshold,"\line   A: "Bring every voice ___________________________"\line   B: "__________________ you have to _____________"\line   C: "_______________________________ that threshold,"\line\line\b IX -- full:\b0  "and let them all speak those words at once, in true need."\line   A: "and let them all speak _______________________________"\line   B: "________________________ those words at once, __________"\line   C: "____________________________________________ in true need."\line\line\b X -- full (this IS Emblem X's own drowned canon text, recovered no other way):\b0  "The Voice is worship - Follow the Inner path - Speak only in True Need"\line   A: "The Voice is worship - ________________________________"\line   B: "________________________ Follow the Inner path - ________"\line   C: "____________________________________________ Speak only in True Need"\f0\fs24\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b THE ASSEMBLED MESSAGE (once all seven are pooled):\cf2\b0  "There is a stair that goes down to the root of this mountain. I did not close it with stone, nor with iron. I closed it with the last stone of the path -- the words cut upon it are the door. Bring every voice you have to that threshold, and let them all speak those words at once, in true need. [Emblem X follows, recovered whole:] The Voice is worship - Follow the Inner path - Speak only in True Need."\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b NOTE -- THE MESSAGE CARRIES NO LOCATION:\cf2\b0  it gives the HOW and nothing else. Jurgen never says where the stair is. The party can assemble every fragment perfectly and still not know where to stand. Only ARNGEIR supplies the WHERE (3C) -- which is why they must go inside the monastery, and why they then have to climb back down.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b THE SOLUTION:\cf2\b0  reading Emblem X at the monastery door COMPLETES the message -- that is the HOW, and they have it standing outside High Hrothgar. Inside, the masters give them the WHERE: the way down is at the wind-tunnel (3B), the place they already fought the troll. They must climb back DOWN to it. There, the FULL travelling company -- not just the PCs -- must stand present and speak Emblem X's line together, at once. "Every voice you have" means every voice, including Ylva, GEAR, and whoever else climbed. Partial attendance or a lone reciter does not open it.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b FAILURE CONSEQUENCES (escalating, matching this Zone's Tempo distortion -- not generic damage):\cf2\b0  First wrong/incomplete recitation: the door does not open, and everyone present takes a SECONDARY HORROR roll (master card, Section 1's box) as broken time punishes the false start. Second failure: same roll, plus the group's next Tempo alarm fires immediately, no warning banked. Third failure: the door stays shut and does not respond to speech again this visit -- they must leave, rest, and return to try once more.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b HINTS (graduated):\cf2\b0\line   Tier 1 (automatic, once two players compare their masks aloud): the stones are showing different words to different people -- not a trick of the light, a pattern.\line   Tier 2 (Standard Guile or Magic): only the corrupted emblems (IV and up) carry a second layer; Emblems I-III show everyone the same thing because they have nothing hidden.\line   Tier 3 (Hard -4 Guile or Magic): a partial recitation, or reciting without everyone present, produces nothing but the failure -- this is a THRESHOLD ritual, not a password typed by one person.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b ALTERNATIVE BYPASS:\cf2\b0  Orion's Diagnostic Song (established 3C -- he reads the corruption as frequency/mechanism) can be aimed at the threshold itself: a Very Hard (-6) Magic roll lets him hear the door's true note directly and guide the recitation without full textual reconstruction. Success on a Magic roll this hard is expensive and rare -- it saves the party from needing every mask, not from needing every voice present.\par

\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw20 \sb60\sa220\par

\pard\sb280\sa40\cf1\b\fs40 SECTION 1   THE APPROACH & GUARDIAN -- the foot of the Steps\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa220\cf3\i ZONE 1. Purpose (per the guide): set the scene and present the first obstacle, confirming the danger. The Steps begin; EMBLEM I still reads clean; the mountain proves it is HELD by something that cheats.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "The Steps begin where the town ends -- grey slabs worn hollow down the middle by seven thousand years of feet, climbing away from the river in long switchbacks that go up into cloud somewhere above the third turn.\line\line The first tablet stands at the bottom of the flight, shoulder-height in its socket at the edge of the stone. The carving is sharp and clean, and every word of it still reads."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  Read EMBLEM I aloud, in full (roster above). It is clean -- the sap has not reached this far down. Let them hear the mountain speak clearly ONCE, so the drowned stones higher up land as a loss.\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD  (as they start up)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "As you start up, the snow around your boots falls UPWARD for three heartbeats -- a soft reversed flurry -- and settles as if it had never moved."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 ZONE TABLES FOR THIS CLIMB (d20 TEMPO -- roll at start of each combat turn)\b0\line\line ZONE 1 -- THE LOWER STEPS, Emblems I-V (alarm ~15 min)\line   1-2 Frozen | 3-5 Dragging | 6-15 In step | 16-19 Quickened | 20 Unmoored\line\line ZONE 2 -- THE UPPER STEPS, Emblems VI-X -> High Hrothgar (alarm ~10 min)\line   1-3 Frozen | 4-7 Dragging | 8-13 In step | 14-18 Quickened | 19-20 Unmoored\line\line Run the alarm honestly -- the dread of the sound is the mechanic.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  ROPE RULE, said out loud once: stay together. Anyone who strays faces the weather ALONE -- this zone's Tempo with nobody to pull them back into the shared now.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf4\b\fs26 THE GUARDIAN -- THE CHEATED GAME (Dagonite ambush at EMBLEM I)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  Use CULTISTS + a ZEALOT (cards, Scene 8). They surf the weather: stolen turns, doubled moves, strikes out of sequence -- while the party rolls Tempo. Make the unfairness VISIBLE. Then let the party break the shards and even the game -- that reversal is the fun, and it TEACHES the tide-shard counter they will need all the way up.\par

\pard\sa140\b YLVA -- THE REFEREE'S WRATH (this develops SAIJAH, not Ylva):\b0  the first time a cultist cheats, Ylva's hackles rise BEFORE it happens -- she growls at empty air a half-second early, then the cheat comes. She cannot name 'temporal weather'; she knows a FOUL happened. "They're cheating. The hunt has RULES." Her fury is theological -- the laws make the game sacred; these people spit on the game itself. Saijah, watching, is seeing the exact hunt-rhythm sense Hircine could one day give HER.\par
\b VARON\b0  (falling in at Ylva's shoulder, blades reversed): "Then we kill them properly. Inside the rules. For once, hound, we agree." She doesn't answer him. Her growl changes register -- and the two who cannot stand each other fight back to back, aligned. Give it one beat out loud.\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 THE GROUND -- the first flight (read this before you place anybody)\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 THE FIRST FLIGHT\b0  -- a straight run of about forty steps from the town edge up\line to the first switchback. Wide enough for THREE abreast, no more. The party's\line column WILL be strung out along it, non-combatants included. That is the point.\line\line\b THE WALL (left going up)\b0  -- bare mountain, too sheer to climb in a hurry.\line No cover on this side.\line\line\b THE FALL (right going up)\b0  -- the slope drops away toward the treeline. It is\line not a killing height: anyone put over the edge tumbles, takes falling damage\line (reference table), and finishes somewhere below the bottom of the flight. The\line real cost is the CLIMB BACK -- they are out of the fight for several rounds.\line\line\b THE LANDING (top of the flight)\b0  -- flat, roughly twenty feet across, where the\line Steps turn back on themselves. Whoever holds it shoots down the whole flight.\line\line\b THE BOULDER FIELD (above and left of the landing)\b0  -- broken rock on the slope,\line waist to head height, good cover, loose underfoot. Reachable off the stair by\line anyone willing to leave it -- Standard (+0) Agility to scramble up into it.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf4\b\fs26 THE AMBUSH -- how they fight it\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b OPENING POSITIONS\cf2\b0  (4 DAGONITE CULTISTS + 1 SHARD ZEALOT -- cards in Scene 8):\par
\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 \b THE ZEALOT holds the landing\b0 , at the top of the flight, and does not leave it. It casts down the stair -- Ash Shell to lock someone in place on open steps, Firebolt at whoever is helping the non-combatants.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 \b TWO CULTISTS in the boulder field\b0 , above and left. They throw loose rock and shoot down into the column, and they have cover the whole time.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 \b TWO CULTISTS come down the flight\b0  to meet the party head-on and hold them on the stair. They do not try to win -- they try to STOP the column moving.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b THE PLAN THEY ARE RUNNING:\cf2\b0  pin the party on a three-wide stair where the line cannot spread, and work it from above. They let the party get well onto the flight before they open. Nobody here is trying to hold ground for its own sake -- they are a picket, and their job is that nothing reaches the top.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b THE CHEAT, MADE VISIBLE (this is what the whole encounter is for):\cf2\b0  every enemy here CHOOSES its Tempo result instead of rolling, via the tide-shard grafted at the sternum. Run it so the table SEES it -- a cultist acts twice before anyone has gone once; the zealot's spell lands before the round it was cast in. Let it be infuriating. Then let them break it.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b THE COUNTER (they must leave this fight knowing it):\cf2\b0  destroy the shard and that enemy rolls Tempo like everyone else -- a Called Shot at it (Very Hard, -6), a Sunder aimed at it, or the bearer's death (it shatters on its own then). The instant one shard goes and that cultist starts rolling, the lesson has landed.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b IF NOBODY WORKS IT OUT IN THE FIGHT:\cf2\b0  they still get it afterward off the bodies -- see the third clue below. Do NOT let the party leave this section without the counter. Every enemy above this point cheats, and the party needs to know why.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b IF THEY TRY TO AVOID THE FIGHT ENTIRELY:\cf2\b0  the picket is watching the stair and the stair is the only way up; slipping the whole column past is not on offer. A scout alone might get by -- and is then SEPARATED, on a mountain where that has a price (see 3A). If the party somehow skips the encounter, hand them the shard-counter another way before Section 3 or the Setback becomes unfair.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 WHAT THE GROUND TELLS THEM (gated clues -- real content, no dead ends)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Notice (Standard, +0) -- the camp behind the landing:\b0  a fire scar burned down to cold ash, bedding rolls, cracked bones and rind from meals. Eight or nine days of it. These men did not follow the party up from Ivarstead -- they have been sitting here.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Notice (Hard, -4) -- which way they were facing:\b0  every position on this ground is set to watch DOWNHILL. The boulder nests look down the flight. The bedding is laid so a man wakes facing the town. Nobody here was watching the mountain above them, and nobody built anything to stop something coming DOWN. They were put here to keep people OUT.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Guile or Magic (Hard, -4) -- a shard, examined on a body:\b0  a sliver of obsidian driven into the breastbone and grown into it, the flesh for two inches around it grey and dead. It is not worn, it is PART of him. Break the shard and whatever it was doing for him stops -- which is what the party may already have proved in the fight, and what they will need on every enemy from here to the undercroft.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 LOOT (Guardian tier, per the campaign creation guide)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Coin:\b0  \b 25-50 gold per player\b0  -- taken off pilgrims, by the look of it: old offering-coin, a few pieces of jewellery that were nobody's on this mountain.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Consumables:\b0  \b 1-2 minor consumables\b0  from the camp -- rations, a cold-draught or two, whatever a picket carries.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Weapons:\b0  jagged glass-steel blades, \b Dmg 20\b0  (cultist card). Ugly, effective, and they mark the carrier as having been up here.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE SEARCH REWARD -- the useful equipment (do not skip this one):\b0  the picket has good gear for holding a mountain. \b A long coil of sound climbing rope and a bag of iron pitons.\b0  The party may already have line; this is better line, and more of it. It matters at the repeating stair and it matters at the broken ledge (3A). Give it to them here and let them work out later what it was for.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 The shards:\b0  shattered on death, dark and inert. No use to anyone here -- though a party that keeps one has kept the first physical proof of how the cult cheats, and Nora would want to look at it.\par

\pard\sb100\sa240\cf1\b EXIT ->\cf2\b0  The ambush is broken; the way up is open. The Steps climb into cloud.   \b -> SECTION 2  THE PUZZLE\b0\par

\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw20 \sb60\sa220\par

\pard\sb280\sa40\cf1\b\fs40 SECTION 2   THE PUZZLE -- the lower climb\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa220\cf3\i ZONE 1 into ZONE 2. Purpose (per the guide): test the party's wits, not their weapons; a change of pace. The temporal weather stops being scenery and becomes a PROBLEM TO SOLVE -- how to move a roped column of non-combatants up a mountain where time itself will not hold still.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b THE PUZZLE, RESOLVED [LOCKED]:\cf2\b0  reading the emblems IS the puzzle -- see THE HIDDEN LAYER, built into the emblem roster above (Part Three intro). It spans Emblems IV through X (this Section starts it at II-V; it finishes at the end of 3C), so it is collected across the whole climb and solved at the monastery threshold. Full solution, failure consequences, hint tiers, and bypass are written there -- do not duplicate them here.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 SAIJAH -- STATIONS 2-3 (paced, never a lecture; hand her player each one as sensation)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Station 2 (deeper):\b0  she PLACES the signature -- "I've felt this before" (Thornheart; the Solitude temple tree) -- but it reads as coincidence. Two points, no pattern. Hers to voice or sit on.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Station 3:\b0  the migraine DOUBLES -- two simultaneous sources, which makes no sense to her. The -2/-2/+2 is fully on. Something is deeply wrong; she does not know what.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM -- CUT AND KEPT OUT [LOCKED]:\cf2\b0  the old draft had a "Station 4" in which GAELEN SPOKE TO SAIJAH through the leylines mid-climb. It is DELETED and must not come back. Two reasons: (1) no mechanism exists in our canon for him to reach her; (2) it contradicts his entire character (`gate 3` lines 121-135 -- warm, sincere, wants them to CHOOSE; his RESTRAINT is the characterization). Gods can reach Saijah -- Kynareth at High Hrothgar, Hircine at the undercroft. Gaelen cannot and would not. First contact with him is FACE TO FACE in Section 4.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 THE OTHERS ON THE CLIMB (each one routed through a player)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 NORA'S GEMS (whoever looks at her during a weather shift):\b0  the two black gems at her throat are stuttering -- the light inside them doubling, like two heartbeats out of step. She's clutching them and saying nothing. A horror beat, not a resolution; whether anyone goes to her is the table's call.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 ALFONSO (carried by ORION this leg):\b0  Orion feels him brace every time the weather shifts -- a flinch in the iron, each time, that he will not explain. If pressed: "Ask me at the bottom of the mountain." (He does not know what broken time does to a soul living in a sword. He does not want to learn it here.)\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 BJORN:\b0  his speed may FLICKER again on the climb if Mila is threatened -- shorter than the road flare, still read as rejuvenation, never named. Davinia's Sight logs one more silent data point if her player looks.\par

\pard\sb100\sa240\cf1\b EXIT ->\cf2\b0  Past EMBLEM V. The air thins, the weather worsens, the switchbacks steepen.   \b -> SECTION 3  EXPLORATION & SETBACK\b0\par

\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw20 \sb60\sa220\par

\pard\sb280\sa40\cf1\b\fs40 SECTION 3   EXPLORATION & SETBACK -- the upper climb, the troll, the monastery\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa220\cf3\i ZONE 2. Purpose (per the guide): deepen the mystery and raise the stakes as the party explores the adventure's core -- a series of interconnected areas ending in a COMPLICATION: the way forward is blocked. Three linked stretches: the upper Steps, the FROST TROLL at the wind-tunnel, then HIGH HROTHGAR -- resupply and the Tower revelation, with no rest on offer -- ending on the block: the summit is sealed, and the working is BELOW them.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf4\b\fs26 3A -- THE UPPER STEPS: WHAT THE MOUNTAIN TAKES\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa220\cf3\i ZONE 2 begins here. This stretch is NOT a fight -- the Guardian was combat and the Setback is combat, and putting a third one between them flattens the whole section. Here the mountain is the antagonist. Three linked stretches, EMBLEMS VI, VII and VIII, and the first real test of the ROPE RULE the party was handed back in Section 1. Everything here costs them something they have to carry into the troll.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD  (crossing into Zone 2)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "Past the fifth tablet the Steps turn steeper and the switchbacks come closer together, and the cloud you have been climbing toward is simply around you now. Sound goes strange in it -- the person in front of you is speaking at a normal volume and arriving quiet, and someone at the back of the line laughs at something and you hear it twice, from two different distances."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  Zone 2 Tempo is live from here (table in Section 1's box; alarm ~10 min). Say the rope rule out loud ONE more time, in fiction, through whoever is leading -- and then hold the party to it for the rest of the climb.\par

\pard\sb140\sa60\cf5\b\fs22 STRETCH ONE -- THE STAIR THAT REPEATS  (EMBLEM VI)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li400\sa160\cf3\i Established canon, not a new invention: pilgrims on this path report walking the same stretch for hours without reaching the summit (`factions/new mythic dawn`). This is that, happening to the party.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "You pass the sixth tablet, and the Steps bend left around a shoulder of rock, and climb, and bend left again around a shoulder of rock -- and the sixth tablet is in front of you, with the same sap standing in the same cuts, and the same three of your own bootprints in the snow beside it going up."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  Read EMBLEM VI (and hand out its masked fragment) the FIRST time only. On every loop after that, it is the same stone and the same words -- do not re-read it, just tell them it is the same stone. Let them loop twice before anyone rolls anything. The party will try things; run them honestly.\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 BREAKING THE LOOP -- the real solutions (any ONE of these works)\b0\line\line\b THE ROPE (no roll -- this is the intended answer):\b0  if the party is actually roped\line together as instructed, they can pay the line out and keep walking. The loop\line cannot close while a single continuous physical object spans it. The line goes\line taut, something on the far end resists for a moment, and then the path lets go.\line THE POINT: the rule they were handed in Section 1 was the answer all along.\line\line\b A FIXED MARK (Standard, +0 -- Guile):\b0  scratch or paint a mark on the tablet and\line walk on. If the mark is there next time, they can count the loops and find the\line one place the path is different -- a step whose wear pattern faces the wrong way.\line Walk off THAT step and the loop breaks.\line\line\b ORION'S EAR (Hard, -4 Magic):\b0  the loop has a seam and it makes a sound at the\line seam. He can hear where the stretch stitches back to its own beginning and walk\line the party through it.\line\line\b LEAVING SOMEONE BEHIND AS A MARKER (works -- and costs):\b0  post one person at\line the tablet and have the rest walk until they come back around to them. It DOES\line break the loop. It also breaks the rope, and the person left standing alone is\line SEPARATED -- run the separation consequence below, in full. The party may well\line think of this first. Let them. Do not warn them.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb140\sa60\cf5\b\fs22 STRETCH TWO -- THE BROKEN STAIR  (EMBLEM VII)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "The seventh tablet stands at the near edge of a gap. Forty feet of the Steps have gone down the mountain -- recently, by the colour of the break -- and what is left is a ledge about as wide as your shoulders running along the rock face, with old iron pilgrim-rings hammered into the stone above it at intervals, most of them still sound."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM -- THIS IS A LOGISTICS PROBLEM, NOT A DEATH TRAP.\cf2\b0  A capable adult crosses it. The point is EVERYONE ELSE: this is where the everyone-climbs decision from Ivarstead presents its bill. Mila cannot cross alone. Esbern is old. GEAR weighs what a Dwemer automaton weighs and the ledge was cut for people. Any animals brought this far cannot use a shoulder-wide ledge at all -- if Mable or Balthazzar are with the party, this is where they must be left, sent back down, or the party finds something clever.\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 The rings are the intended tool:\b0  rope through the pilgrim-rings makes a handline. Crossing roped is a Standard (+0) Agility check for anyone able-bodied, and no check at all for anyone secured and led.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Unroped, in a Zone 2 wind:\b0  Hard (-4) Agility. Do not let them try it unroped without saying the wind out loud first.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 GEAR:\b0  the ledge will take his weight or it will not, and nobody can know which. His player and the party decide. If he goes across and it holds, it holds. If the party sends him back, he goes without argument and rejoins them nowhere -- he simply is not there for the troll, and that is a real loss.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 TIME COST:\b0  moving a column this size across a shoulder-wide ledge takes as long as it takes. Fire the Zone 2 alarm AT LEAST once during the crossing. That is the cost -- not damage, TIME, and time here is the weather.\par

\pard\sb140\sa60\cf5\b\fs22 STRETCH THREE -- WHAT THE WEATHER LEFT  (EMBLEM VIII)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "Three men in ash-grey robes are lying on the Steps around the eighth tablet, in the open, arranged the way people fall rather than the way people are placed. There is no blood on the stone and no weapon anywhere near them. The snow has not covered them yet."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Notice (Standard, +0):\b0  no wounds. Not one, on any of the three. Their robes are whole. Whatever killed them never touched them.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Notice (Hard, -4):\b0  the three did not die the same way. One is an old man wearing a young man's clothes, hands knotted with age that his face has not caught up to. One is starved to the bone inside robes that still fit him. The third is neither -- unmarked, unaged, and simply dead.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Notice or Magic (Hard, -4) on the bodies:\b0  each one has an obsidian tide-shard grafted at the sternum, and every shard is dark and cracked through. (The party may already know from the Guardian fight that a shard shatters when its bearer dies -- these are consistent with that, not evidence that the shards failed first.)\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b WHAT THIS ACTUALLY TELLS THEM (GM -- let them assemble it, do not say it):\cf2\b0  the weather up here does not do ONE thing. It did three different things to three men standing together. That is the same principle the emblems have been running on all climb -- each person in their own now -- and it is the first time the party sees it kill. If a player says it out loud, confirm it. If nobody does, it lands later.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b LOOT:\cf2\b0  what three cultists carry -- a little coin, rations, and their jagged glass-steel blades (Dmg 20 each, per the cultist card in Scene 8). The shards are inert and worthless now; the intact ones only come from the undercroft (Section 5's reward loot).\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 THE SEPARATION CONSEQUENCE -- what "SEPARATION IS EXPOSURE" actually costs\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 FIRES whenever anyone leaves the roped line in Zone 2 -- by choice, by being sent,\line by wandering off to search, or by being posted somewhere as a marker.\b0\line\line They are out of contact for what the REST of the party experiences as a minute or\line two. What THEY experience is hours: walking, in the snow, in the dark, on a stair\line that keeps arriving at itself, calling out and being answered a long time later in\line their own voice.\line\line\b MECHANICAL RESULT: EXHAUSTED\b0  -- a -2 penalty to ALL Stat Scores (Target Numbers),\line lasting until a LONG REST. (Established condition, same terms as the forced march.)\line\line\b WHY THIS HURTS SO MUCH HERE:\b0  there is NO rest anywhere on this mountain. High\line Hrothgar is resupply only -- the working never stops running, so the party never\line gets an hour. Anyone separated in Zone 2 carries the -2 through the FROST TROLL,\line through the monastery, back down the mountain, and into the UNDERCROFT. It does\line not come off until the module is over.\line\line\b IT IS MEANT TO BE DEBILITATING. DO NOT SOFTEN IT.\b0  The rope rule was said out loud\line twice before this could ever fire, and the fiction warned them every step. A party\line that breaks formation on this mountain carries it to the end. Deliver it flatly,\line without apology, and let them feel the whole climb differently afterward.\line\line\b DO NOT KILL ANYONE WITH THIS.\b0  It is a tax on breaking formation, not a trap. Deliver\line the hours privately to that player, in as much detail as you like, while the rest of\line the table waits -- the silence at the table is part of the cost.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb100\sa240\cf1\b EXIT ->\cf2\b0  Past the eighth tablet the path narrows toward a gap between the rock and a long overhang, and the wind coming out of it is loud enough to hear from here.   \b -> 3B  THE WIND TUNNEL\b0\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf4\b\fs26 3B -- THE WIND TUNNEL: THE THING THAT CANNOT FINISH\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa220\cf3\i ZONE 2. The SETBACK. Three things stand in this stretch of path: EMBLEM IX, drowned; a flat face of rock that is actually Jurgen's sealed door, which the party has no way to recognise; and the troll. They will fight here on the doorstep of the thing they came for, and not know it. THE PARTY COMES BACK HERE at the end of 3C to open the door -- build the space so it works twice.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "The Steps run into a gap between a wall of rock on your left and a long shelf of overhanging stone on your right, and the wind comes through it hard enough to lean on. Snow moves sideways in it. You can see perhaps twenty feet ahead and no further, and everything past that is white and moving.\line\line The ninth tablet stands at the mouth of the gap with black sap standing in every cut of it. Past that the path widens a little where the overhang deepens into a shallow cave, and the wind drops there, and the snow lies undisturbed.\line\line Something in that sheltered dark stands up, and it keeps standing up, and it is taller than the opening it is standing in."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM -- THE DOOR IS HERE AND THEY MUST NOT KNOW IT:\cf2\b0  the far wall of the sheltered cave is Jurgen's door. Describe it, if they look, as exactly what it appears to be -- a flat face of rock with old carving under a skin of sap. There is no seam, no handle, no hinge and no mechanism, because Jurgen closed it with neither stone nor iron. A Notice or Investigation check here is not a trap and is not gated: they can search it as thoroughly as they like and the honest answer is that there is nothing there to find. Do NOT hint. Do NOT let a good roll flag it. The reveal belongs to Arngeir in 3C, and the return trip is the payoff.\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 TERRAIN -- THE WIND TUNNEL (this is half the encounter; run it strictly)\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 THE OPEN GAP (most of the space)\b0\line   - FOUR OR FIVE can stand abreast. Movement is limited, not a squeeze.\line   - VISIBILITY ~20 ft. Beyond that the snow is a wall. Ranged attacks past\line     20 ft are made blind -- the GM applies the Difficulty Modifier ladder as\line     usual for shooting at what you cannot see.\line   - BURNING EFFECTS LAST HALF AS LONG (round UP). The wind tears fire off\line     whatever it is on. A 3-round burn is 2 rounds out here.\line\line\b THE OVERHANG / THE DEN (the sheltered pocket, the troll's ground)\b0\line   - OUT OF THE WIND. BURNING EFFECTS LAST THEIR FULL DURATION here.\line   - It is the ONLY ground on this mountain where fire behaves.\line   - It is also the troll's den, it is enclosed, and the far wall is the door.\line\line\b THE CHOICE THIS FORCES (do not explain it at the table; let them find it):\b0\line   Fight in the open and keep room to move -- and watch every burn gutter out\line   early. Fight in the den and your fire finally works -- in an enclosed pocket,\line   in reach of a thing with more arms than it needs, with nowhere to fall back to.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb120\sa50\cf4\b\f1\fs20 THE TROLL -- statistics and the three bodies\cf2\b0\f0\fs24\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 FROST TROLL, DAGON-WARPED -- TL 3 BOSS (Expert tier, party AP~12)\b0\line Stats: 19 / 17 / 13 / 8   (P = Might)\line HP 900, split into THREE PHASE BARS of 300  |  Dmg 41  |  DR 22 (bar 1)\line VILLAIN ACTION (Boss Mechanics): one extra action at the end of every second\line player's turn. Damage does NOT carry between bars.\line\line\b FIRE: ALL fire damage against it is DOUBLED, in every bar.\b0  It is a frost troll\line and it never stops being one underneath.\line\line\b IT DOES NOT REGENERATE TURN BY TURN.\b0  Nothing to track. Its healing IS the\line phase transition: when a bar breaks it does not recover, it becomes the next\line thing. That is the whole of its regeneration and the whole of its horror.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb140\sa60\cf5\b\fs22 BAR 1 (900 -> 601) -- THE TROLL\cf2\b0\fs24\par
\pard\li400\sa160\cf3\i White pelt, three eyes, black sap crusted into the fur of its shoulders and back. It fights exactly like what it is.\cf2\i0\par
\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 ONE attack per turn, Dmg 41. Grabs and mauls; drags prey toward the den.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 IMMUNE to frost. Fire doubles.  DR 22.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 It fights like an animal defending its ground -- it goes for whoever is closest and whoever is hurting it most, and it will break off to protect the den mouth.\par

\pard\sb140\sa60\cf5\b\fs22 BAR 2 (600 -> 301) -- TOO MUCH ARM\cf2\b0\fs24\par
\pard\li400\sa160\cf3\i It heals wrong. What comes up off the snow has arms it did not have, in places arms do not go, and the new flesh is pale and unfinished and has not grown a hide yet.\cf2\i0\par
\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 TWO attacks per turn, Dmg 41 each.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 Still IMMUNE to frost. Fire still doubles.  \b DR drops to 14\b0  -- the new limbs have no hide on them, and the party can see which parts are soft.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 Still fighting like a troll, and still fighting well. This bar is the DANGEROUS one: it has doubled its output and it has not yet lost the ability to aim.\par

\pard\sb140\sa60\cf5\b\fs22 BAR 3 (300 -> 1) -- THE LOOP THAT WILL NOT STOP RUNNING\cf2\b0\fs24\par
\pard\li400\sa160\cf3\i The behaviour is intact. The body is not. It is still hunting, still defending its den, still doing every single thing a troll does -- on a shape that can no longer do any of them.\cf2\i0\par
\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 THREE attacks per turn, Dmg 41 each. It targets normally, like any animal fighting a group -- nearest, or whoever is hurting it most.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b THE BODY ANSWERS WRONG -- roll d20 for EACH attack:\b0\line      \b 11+\b0  the limb it aimed with is the limb that moves. It hits its target.\line      \b 6-10\b0  a different limb answers. Hits a RANDOM other creature in reach\line             (friend, foe, or one of the party's own animals -- roll it honestly).\line      \b 1-5\b0  it strikes rock, snow, or itself. No damage to anyone. Describe it.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 ALL resistances and immunities are GONE, frost included -- nothing about it holds one shape long enough to resist anything. Fire STILL doubles.  \b DR 10.\b0\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b NO WOUNDED STATE, NO MORALE.\b0  It does not stagger, flinch, slow, or react to injury at any total. It fights at 1 HP exactly as it fought at 300. Nothing the party does registers, because nothing registers.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b NO FACING.\b0  Flanking and positional advantage do not apply. It does not have a back for long enough to get behind.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM -- LETHALITY DIAL:\cf2\b0  three Boss-tier attacks in a turn is a lot against this system's HP values. You hold the sheets -- if the party's DR spread cannot absorb it, drop Bar 3 to TWO attacks and keep everything else. The d20 targeting table is the part that matters; the attack count is the dial.\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD  (bar 1 breaks -- it heals into Bar 2)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "It goes down on one knee in the snow and the wound closes -- and keeps closing, and keeps going after there is nothing left to close. An arm comes out below the arm it already has. Another one starts at the shoulder blade. The new flesh is the colour of the inside of a mouth. It puts all of them on the ground and stands up on more limbs than it went down with."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD  (bar 2 breaks -- it heals into Bar 3)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "The second body splits at the shoulders and the thing inside it keeps rising, and keeps rising. An arm grows down its side, past the snow, and ends in nothing. The three eyes close over; two open on its back. It turns a sliding face toward you -- and the sound coming out of it is the same note it started making at the first blow, unbroken since.\line\line It reaches for you with a leg."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM -- WHAT THIS SCENE IS FOR (do not say any of it out loud):\cf2\b0  this is Gaelen's doctrine performed in front of the party before he ever gets to pitch it. In Section 4 he tells them the Aedra gave mortals a CELL of linear time and fragile flesh, that Dagon offers the KEY, and that the Unmaking is HEALING. The party will already have watched something be released from fixed form. It could not finish being anything, it could not stop, and it never once stopped making that sound. When he says the word "healing," they have a referent for it and he does not get to define the term. Play the callback silently -- do not have anyone point it out.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 LOOT -- THE DEN\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  Pilgrims have used this path for seven thousand years and the troll has denned here for some of it. The sheltered pocket holds what is left of the ones who did not get past it -- packs gone to rot, a few frozen intact.\par
\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 \b 30-60 gold per player\b0  in old coin and pilgrim offerings (Setback-tier loot, per the campaign creation guide).\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 \b One useful consumable per two players\b0  -- travel remedies, cold-weather draughts, whatever a pilgrim carried up a mountain.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 \b Creative-solution bonus:\b0  if the party solved the fire/wind problem cleverly rather than by grinding through it, add a piece of real equipment from the frozen packs. GM's pick.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 \b GM note:\b0  the far wall of this den is the door. The party will loot this room with their hands on it and not know. Say nothing.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 THE RETURN VISIT (after 3C -- the same ground, silent)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  They come back down here with the assembled message and Arngeir's directions. The troll is dead where they left it. The wind is still running. Nothing attacks them. Run the recitation (full solution, failure consequences and hint tiers are in THE HIDDEN LAYER, Part Three intro) in the quiet, standing in the blood and the snow of a fight they already won -- everyone present, every voice, Emblem X's line spoken at once. Do not add a second encounter here. The contrast is the payoff.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf4\b\i TODO -- BATTLEMAP:\cf2\b0  this fight needs a drawn map. Required features: the gap (4-5 abreast), the rock wall, the overhang shelf, the sheltered den pocket, the 20 ft visibility ring, Emblem IX at the mouth, and the door as the den's far wall (unmarked on any player-facing copy).\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf4\b\i 3C: HIGH HROTHGAR\cf2\b0  -- the existing monastery material follows below, and is the back half of THIS section: the rest/resupply beat plus the Tower revelation, ending on the way forward being blocked. NOTE: the Greybeards have little use for potions (each is Minor Dragon-tier and the Thu'um costs no magicka) -- resupply is thin and flavorful unless the party carried Klimmek's pack up and asks.\i0\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf4\b\fs26 3C -- HIGH HROTHGAR: THE ORDER THAT WILL NOT COME DOWN\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa220\cf3\i The resupply stop -- NOT a rest -- and the coldest scene in the module. FIVE Greybeards: ARNGEIR, EINARTH, BORRI and WULFGAR in the monastery, and PAARTHURNAX their grandmaster at the summit -- sealed behind weather only a Shout clears, unreachable this module. Only ARNGEIR speaks; the other three do not speak at all. The party gets rest, resupply, and the WHERE. What they do not get is help.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b THE SPINE OF THIS SCENE (hold it):\cf2\b0  the order is NOT torn. There is no faction to win over and no argument in progress. They considered whether the unmaking of the mountain constitutes a True Need, they ruled that it does not, and the matter is closed. Arngeir will answer any question the party asks, completely and without evasion -- because TELLING IS NOT DOING. He hands them the door, the words, and the history, and does not move one step toward it himself. Play him calm, not agonized. The horror is that he is at peace.\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "High Hrothgar is one long block of grey stone set into the mountain where the Steps end, and there are no windows in the face of it. The last tablet stands beside the doors, under a skin of black sap standing in every cut of the carving.\line\line The doors are shut. They are not barred. They swing when pushed.\line\line Inside is a single hall, colder than the open air you just left. A firepit at the center holds no fire and no ash -- swept out, and left that way. Four old men in grey robes sit on the floor at wide intervals, none of them near another, none of them near the pit. They do not look up when the doors open. They do not look up when the doors close.\line\line One of them stands."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  EMBLEM X is here, at the doors, fully drowned. If the party has been collecting the hidden layer, this is where the last masked fragment is handed out and the message COMPLETES -- outside, before they ever go in. They now have the HOW and no idea where to use it.\par

\pard\sa140\b ARNGEIR\b0  (first words; he is looking at the pack, not at their faces): "Is that Klimmek's?"\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  He does not greet them and does not ask who they are. If they hand the pack over, he takes it in both hands and carries it to the three seated men before he says anything further; they eat where they sit, without speaking. Only then does he come back. If the party did NOT bring the pack, he says "Then no," sets the matter down, and the scene proceeds -- colder and shorter, and he gives the same information anyway.\par

\pard\sa140\b ARNGEIR:\b0  "Nobody has come up in some weeks. Tell me what is down there."\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM -- HE IS STARVED FOR NEWS AS WELL AS FOOD.\cf2\b0  Let the party report Ivarstead, the cultists, the troll, whatever they choose to tell him. He listens all the way through without interrupting. Whatever they say, he receives it the way a man receives weather -- not as a call to action.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 WHAT ARNGEIR GIVES FREELY (he answers everything; asking costs the party nothing)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 WHAT IS HAPPENING TO THE MOUNTAIN:\b0  "The Throat is one of the pillars that hold the world up. Something at its root is cutting into it." If asked how he knows: "We have sat on it our whole lives. You would know too."\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE WHERE -- the door (this is the beat the whole section exists for):\b0  "There is a door below you. On the path, where the wind runs between the overhang and the rock." (a pause) "You will have walked past it."\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE HOW -- he knows the mechanism and says so plainly:\b0  "It opens to many voices at once, spoken in true need." (a pause) "We are four, and three of us do not speak at all."\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE SUMMIT:\b0  sealed by weather no one here can walk through; only a Shout clears it. Their grandmaster is up there. Arngeir confirms this if asked and will not discuss it further. NO Paarthurnax reveal in this module -- if the party pushes, he stops answering.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 WHAT THE MASTERS CANNOT DO:\b0  no special senses of any kind. They do not see Ismara in Davinia. They sense nothing whatsoever in Bjorn -- nobody can sense a Dragonborn, and no beat in this scene goes near it. They know Word Wall locations and history. That is the whole of their gift.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf4\b\fs26 THE REFUSAL (short, calm, final -- do not let him make a speech of it)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa140 If the party asks the Greybeards to come down, to fight, to open the door themselves, or to use the Voice at all:\par

\pard\sa140\b ARNGEIR:\b0  "No."\par

\pard\sa140 If pressed:\par

\pard\sa140\b ARNGEIR:\b0  "We will not go down."\par

\pard\sa140 If asked whether this is a True Need -- the question the whole order turns on:\par

\pard\sa140\b ARNGEIR:\b0  "We considered it. The answer was no."\par

\pard\sa140 If asked why, and only if asked directly:\par

\pard\sa140\b ARNGEIR:\b0  "The Tongues went to Red Mountain with the Voice at its full strength, and they came away humbled. Jurgen sat with that for seven years before he understood it." (a pause) "He chose silence. We keep it."\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  That is as far as he goes. He does not apologize, does not agonize, does not ask them to understand, and does not stop them leaving. If a player calls it cowardice to his face, he does not defend himself -- he waits until they are finished and then answers the next practical question they ask. Nothing the party says moves him and no roll changes it. This is not a persuasion scene; do not offer one.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 RESUPPLY -- AND WHY THEY CANNOT STOP HERE\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THERE IS NO REST HERE. NONE. NOT EVEN A SHORT ONE.\b0  A Short Rest is an HOUR, and the party does not have an hour -- the working in the undercroft is running the entire time they are standing in this hall. If they bed down, if they take a Short Rest, if they sleep: GAELEN FINISHES. The ritual completes, he is gone, and the module's ending changes to a failure state. Say the pressure out loud in fiction if you have to, through Arngeir or through the floor shaking, but do NOT let the table treat this as a safe house. They can walk in, talk, trade, and walk out. That is all this place offers.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE STORES (offered if the party brought the pack, or if they ask):\b0  ARNGEIR: "There are stores. Pilgrims left things -- remedies, mostly. We have no use for them." What is on the shelves: healing draughts and restoratives left as offerings over years, and dried stores four old men cannot get through. GM: hand out a modest allotment of HEALTH and FP potions priced from your own item tables -- enough to matter going into Section 4, not enough to trivialise it. If the party did NOT bring Klimmek's pack, halve it: what is left is what a hungry monastery can spare.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE TRADE IS THE POINT:\b0  the party feeds a starving order and is repaid in medicine and information by men who will not lift a hand for them. Let it sit without comment.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 KYNARETH (Saijah -- station 5; the goddess, NOT the masters, and NOT about anyone else)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  This is Kyne's own mountain, and it is the first time in the campaign the goddess gives Saijah something that is not an instruction. Hand her PLAYER the sensation and nothing else: standing in this hall, the two sources of her migraine line up for a moment the way two notes struck together make a third -- and for that moment she feels, completely and with no words attached, that the thing under this mountain and the thing under Eldergleam are the SAME KIND OF THING. Then it slips, and the pain comes back doubled. Nothing is named. No conclusion is supplied. What she makes of it is hers, and she may make nothing of it yet -- what this plants does not come due until station 7, on the road out.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 THE OTHERS AT THE MONASTERY (each routed through a player, each short)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 DAVINIA/ISMARA -- the only eyes that can READ it:\b0  hand her player the picture, not a verdict: the leyline lattice of the Tower, and the shape of the corruption running down into its root. She can also see what her own gift could do here -- freeze the wound locally, stasis set against chaos. Put the option on the table once, plainly, and run whatever she chooses. GM-side only: using it advances the CDI. Do not push it, do not forbid it.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 ORION -- the tonal architect:\b0  the corruption is a frequency phenomenon and he is the one person here equipped to read it as MECHANISM. A Diagnostic Song in this hall maps the working's structure -- this is what unlocks his Very Hard (-6) counter-note option at the anchors in Section 4, and it feeds his Severance research. GM-only, say nothing: the Ring has fed Valerius every moment of this climb, live.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 ESBERN:\b0  the first library he has stood in since the Ratway, and these records run back further than the Blades' own. Give him one real find and let his player decide what to do with it. He wants the Dragonstone and will say so again on the road out.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 GEAR:\b0  he climbed, because everyone climbed. He measures the distortion in this hall and reports the figure flatly to whoever is listening. Keep him brief and keep the joke off it.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE THREE WHO DO NOT SPEAK:\b0  Einarth, Borri and Wulfgar are present in every beat of this scene and say nothing in any of them. They eat if fed. They do not rise, do not gesture, and do not acknowledge the party leaving. Do not give any of them a line -- not one word, not for any reason. Their silence is the scene.\par

\pard\sb100\sa240\cf1\b EXIT ->\cf2\b0  THE BLOCK (the Setback lands here): the summit is sealed, and the way down is not in this monastery at all -- it is back at the wind-tunnel, where they fought the troll. Everything they climbed for is behind them. They go back down.   \b -> BACK TO 3B, then SECTION 4  THE CLIMAX\b0\par

\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw20 \sb60\sa220\par

\pard\sb280\sa40\cf1\b\fs40 SECTION 4   THE CLIMAX -- the ritual in the undercroft, Gaelen at the root\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa220\cf3\i The boss. ZONE 3 -- the worst weather. Purpose (per the guide): the dramatic final confrontation against the adventure's primary antagonist. Objective: DISRUPT THE RITUAL. Gaelen escapes by design; Jasper does not. This is the party's FIRST contact with Gaelen -- face to face, as an invitation.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "The undercroft is older than the monastery above it -- a vaulted root-cellar of the world, columns of living rock, wide as their cart, running down into shadow. Red light comes up out of channels carved into the floor with repeating Daedric script, all of them running inward to a central well where the mountain's root stands exposed -- a pillar as wide as High Hrothgar's whole front wall, rock too dense to have grain or seam, veined now with fresh black cracks where the sap has forced its way through, climbing toward an array of obsidian shards arranged in a blooming spiral.\line\line A Bosmer moves through the array unhurried, crimson chitin catching the light, one arm a grafted mass of charred heartwood and obsidian that gestures at things he is not looking at. He is adjusting each shard with the care of a man tuning an instrument he loves.\line\line Without turning, in a voice that is warm and genuinely pleased: 'Oh -- visitors. Good. I hoped the mountain would manage to be heard. Come in, come in. You are not interrupting; nothing can interrupt this. But company is rare, and I have wanted to meet you for the longest time.'"\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  If a player can read Daedric or thinks to try: the carved script repeats one line -- "THE TOWER TOUCHES ALL THE MANTLES OF HEAVEN" -- lifted straight from the Mysterium Xarxes commentary (The Dawn Is Yours, Section VIII). Confirms, in the players' own hands rather than Esbern's mouth, that this ritual and the doctrine he described are the same order, two hundred years apart.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 GAELEN -- HOW HE PLAYS (unsettling, never evil)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 GAELEN DOES NOT FIGHT THIS ENCOUNTER.\b0  He spends the whole scene trying to bring them over, not defending himself -- Jasper is the one who fights, and the anchors are the only obstacle. If a player attacks him directly, he does not retaliate. He keeps talking.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2\b0 The warm, sincere, reasonable CENTER of an apocalypse. He talks like an ally trying to get compatriots to stop suffering for a stagnant world: the Aedra gave mortals a CELL -- linear time, fragile flesh; Dagon offers the KEY; the Unmaking is HEALING.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE QUIET WISH (plant it -- overheard when he thinks no one listens, to no one):\b0  "I want it to be perfect. Please... let it be perfect." Not a plea, not a crack in a mask -- the closest thing his altered architecture has to hope. Every projection the players make onto it will be slightly wrong. He should leave the table unable to dismiss him.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE YLVA EXCHANGE (give both liturgies one beat, out loud):\b0  GAELEN: "Rules are the bars, hunter. I am unbending them." YLVA (shaking with a fury even she cannot name): "The rules are what make the hunt HOLY. Prey runs, hunter chases, the fastest wins -- that is the whole church. You are not freeing anything. You are pissing on the game." The most theologically serious person in the room about why the Unmaking is wrong is the werewolf.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 ALFONSO -- the duel only he can have (Orion, carrying him this leg, feels it land through the working before a word is said):\b0  GAELEN (finding the rapier in the room without being shown it): "You of all things know the cage, brother. Rot is the door. Help me open it." ALFONSO (aloud, after a pause one beat too long -- the pause IS the almost): "Decay FEEDS, gardener. The leaf rots so the root eats. Yours starves. You are not opening a door -- you are salting the field it stood in." Quiet horror for Orion's player to sit with: how close his own ally's philosophy runs to the abyss, and how long he took to answer.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 IF A PLAYER NAMES MYTHIC DAWN / CAMORAN TO HIM:\b0  His hands keep working the shards while he answers, unhurried. "Mythic Dawn. Yes." A beat. "Does the name trouble you more than anything I've actually said?"\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 HIRCINE'S TEMPTATION (Saijah -- station 6, in the doorway of the boss)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  Just before or during the fight, the Hunter's voice -- the morning after Kynareth finally spoke: "Little sentinel. That noise in your skull. I can make it STOP." He is not lying. THE HIDDEN COST he simply does not mention: the channel that screams is the same channel the goddess speaks through -- silence the pain and you silence KYNARETH (the Mzinchaleft principle: silence is not always peace). NO weredog offer, NO choice resolved here -- the senses may sharpen a moment (a taste), and the offer HANGS. If the player accepts outright anyway, do not block it -- but that is her player firing the crossroads early, not the module.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf4\b\fs26 THE FIGHT -- DISRUPT THE WORKING\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa140\b Frame it out loud (again): the objective is the RITUAL.\b0  The spiral array feeds the sap-lines into the root. Break enough of it and the working collapses. Gaelen defends the work, not himself; Jasper defends GAELEN.\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 THE ARRAY:\b0  5 anchor-shards (obsidian, waist-high). Each: \b 25 HP, DR 10\b0  -- or a \b Hard Might (-4)\b0  wrench to topple one bodily, or Orion's tonal counter-note (\b Hard Magic -4\b0 , guided by his Section 3C mapping) to crack one at range. Every anchor down weakens the weather one step in here (Zone 3 -> 2 -> 1...); at THREE down the working is DISRUPTED (win condition); all five down = staunched as clean as this ever gets.\par

\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 ZONE 3 TEMPO, restated for this fight (alarm ~6 min): 1-4 Frozen | 5-8 Dragging | 9-12 In step | 13-17 Quickened | 18-20 Unmoored. Any natural 1 or 20 also triggers a SECONDARY HORROR -- d20 on the master card's sub-table (delayed resolution / retroactive damage / echo-step / doubled-then-lost turn / initiative reshuffle). The cultists cheat it, the party endures it, and every anchor destroyed evens the game a step -- the mechanical arc IS the story arc.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\box\brdrdash\brdrw15 \li300\ri300\sb40\sa220\b\f1\fs20 GAELEN THE ROOT-TWISTER (Apex Villain -- full sheet: Npcs/new mythic dawn leader)\b0\line Magic 21 (cannot fail a Magic roll)  Agility 17  Guile 15  Might 8\line HP 40 (Paradise Mantle: regen -- outheal or shut off)  |  FP 35  |  DR 22 (23 vs spells)\line Mythic Dawn chitin skin; Daedric graft-arm acts on its own initiative.\line HE IS NOT HERE TO DIE: at disruption (3 anchors down) he PHASES -- the collapsing\line weather releases his half-untimed soul and he falls back to the Eldergleam plan.\line If somehow cornered before that, he spends everything to phase early.\line These stats are for escape only -- he never initiates an attack this encounter.\line\line\b JASPER AVALON (lieutenant -- ~AP 10 character build; he does NOT retreat)\b0\line Dunmer mage, THE ATRONACH. Might 4  Agility 6  Magic 19  Guile 12\line HP 9 (glass -- his defense is absorption and ash)  |  FP 16  |  DR 8 (Ebonyflesh up)\line SPELL ABSORPTION (Atronach stone): once per combat, negates a hostile spell\line and regains its FP cost.  Ancestor's Wrath (Dunmer): 1/adventure fire aura.\line Casts: Incinerate (9 FP, 12 dmg) | Ash Shell (4 FP, Might roll or encased 2 rds)\line | Ash Rune (10 FP) | Lightning Bolt (3 FP). Tide-shard graft: cheats Tempo.\line FIGHTS TO THE END defending the ritual space. Dies here. One-and-done.\f0\fs24\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf4\b\fs26 THE JASPER REVEAL (via Gaelen -- this is how the consent lands loud)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  Present the recognition to Davinia's player first and plainly: Davinia KNEW this man -- Jasper Avalon, dead in the Pale Lady's tomb -- and Ismara holds that memory. What she does with recognizing him is hers. Then GAELEN (mild, without pausing his work): "It seems you were familiar with this vessel... that is unfortunate, for he is gone. As for his flesh -- he gave it to the Dawn."\par

\pard\sa140 Jasper was a secret Dagon devotee IN LIFE -- a lone worshipper, long before he died in the Pale Lady's tomb. Nothing was stolen. If the party tries to TALK to him, he CAN speak -- and what he says is worse than silence: he is content. The empty, discarded man was told his emptiness was HOLY, and he gave himself gladly, because it was the first time anything ever wanted him.\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  VICTIM vs WILLING is the differentiator from the Echo: Kyboh was stolen (rage FOR him); Jasper GAVE himself (nothing to save). Do not soften either half.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf4\b\fs26 GAELEN'S ESCAPE (at 3 anchors down -- run it exactly)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "The third anchor cracks and the whole undercroft LURCHES -- the red light guttering, the sap-lines withering back out of the root like ivy burned at the stem. Gaelen watches it happen with his head tilted, the way a man watches weather ruin a picnic.\line\line 'Hm. Unfinished, then.' He looks at you -- genuinely, unhurriedly interested. 'You finally showed up. Do you know, I had begun to think no one was coming, all these years. How wonderful. Something new.'\line\line The broken time reaches for him and he steps INTO it, the way you would step into a doorway -- and he is a half-second behind himself, and then a full second, and then he is an afterimage the room forgets to keep."\cf2\i0\par

\pard\li360\sa140\cf1\b GM:\cf2\b0  He falls back to the ELDERGLEAM -- the primary plan the party has never touched, still running perfectly. They did not beat him; they interrupted a man who reached for a second prize. He recurs with a grievance now attached -- curious, not vengeful. Scarier.\par

\pard\sb100\sa240\cf1\b EXIT ->\cf2\b0  The weather stills. The mountain stops shaking. It is not healed.   \b -> SECTION 5  THE REWARD & RESOLUTION\b0\par

\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw20 \sb60\sa220\par

\pard\sb280\sa40\cf1\b\fs40 SECTION 5   THE REWARD & RESOLUTION -- staunched, not sealed\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\sa220\cf3\i The curtain. Purpose (per the guide): a satisfying payoff and narrative closure -- material reward AND the story's landing. Rewards, the seeds that finish growing AFTER, and the doors this module opens.\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb80\sa60\cf6\b\fs26 >> READ ALOUD\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\li600\ri400\sa220\cf6\i "The masters come down the inner stair when the shaking stops -- slowly, holding the walls, men walking after weeks at an oar. Arngeir stands a long time at the wounded root, where the black lines have withered to scars in the stone.\line\line 'Staunched,' he says at last. 'A cut finger, bound. The body of the thing that attacked this mountain is still out there, and still working.' He looks at each of you in turn, and bows -- the full bow, to all of you. 'The Way of the Voice is open to you. It is a small thing to offer the people who held up the sky. It is what we have.'"\cf2\i0\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 REWARDS\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 1 AP each\b0  (a full fire completed) -- plus dragon-kill AP if any dragon somehow died this module (it should not have).\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 The Way of the Voice:\b0  High Hrothgar open as sanctuary + scholarship; the masters know WHERE Word Walls are for specific shouts a seeker might want (they cannot shout them; reference, not power).\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Loot:\b0  the zealots' tide-shards, inert now -- crystallized wrong-time, priceless to a researcher and mildly unwholesome to carry. NORA wants them badly and says so out loud. Whether ISMARA wants them is her player's business -- just make sure the player knows what they are. Ivarstead half-returns as word spreads the mountain has quieted.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 SEEDS PLANTED (they finish growing AFTER the module -- do not harvest early)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Saijah's realization (station 7, ON THE ROAD OUT, days later):\b0  gildergreens + the Throat + the word TOWER + TWO migraines finally assemble -- one tree, it is a Tower, TWO Towers are under attack, and there is a HEART she has never found. The module plants; the realization grows. Let her player say it first if they get there.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 The heart-hunt -> DANICA:\b0  the true Eldergleam's location is the chosen sisters' deepest secret (a women's mystery -- no man may ever know). Three living sisters: Danica, one in Whiterun (cold to the Lanterns -- Danica's letters made the party the temple's cautionary tale), one visiting Solitude. The road to the cure runs through the reconciliation. The braid is now load-bearing.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 The two cure-routes (set up, never forced):\b0  KYNARETH -- heal the heart (needs Danica). HIRCINE -- no Danica needed at all: the Alpha and the cull. If reconciliation looks impossible, Hircine's road becomes the shortcut priced in Saijah's soul.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Node-saving (the standing open door):\b0  every gildergreen node is SAVEABLE; the party has simply kept not going. From here on, offer node-defense chances on the road; the Solitude node (slow, strong, a chosen sister already present) is the best first savable win.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Valerius knows everything\b0  the Ring saw -- the Tower, the apocalypse, the party's capabilities. File it; spend it later.\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Gaelen, unfinished:\b0  "you finally showed up." The Eldergleam campaign continues off-screen. He is curious about them now. That is worse.\par

\pard\sb160\sa70\cf5\b\fs26 CONSEQUENCE LEDGER (what the OTHER fires did while they climbed)\cf2\b0\fs24\par

\pard\fi-280\li500\sa120\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Fire A (Krusp & Danica at Windhelm):\b0  rumor arrives -- the plague fought to a standstill at a cost. Danica's cough, when they finally hear her again, has teeth in it. [Attrition canon: her lungs.]\par
\cf5\b\bullet\tab\cf2 Fire C (ignored -- Bleak Falls):\b0  Antoine rebuilds unobserved on top of the Dragonstone. The 6-8 week Strain 2.0 clock RUNS. Esbern says the Dragonstone line again on the road out. He will keep saying it.\par

\pard\sb100\sa240\cf1\b EXIT ->\cf2\b0  The cart rolls down out of the grey hills with the mountain quiet behind it -- and the Dragonstone pressure, the Dirge reckoning, and the road to Bleak Falls ahead.   \b -> NEXT MODULE: FIRE C / BLEAK FALLS\b0\par
